var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"feasibility_gate/#Feasibility-before-droop-replacement","page":"Feasibility qualification","text":"Use qualify_droop_replacement before accepting a proposed set of fixed droop curves for independent-demand study. It checks the calibration point, then the source and candidate models at every required demand factor. All candidate curves are present together. A point that passes each generator separately does not establish that the combined system is feasible.\n\nDecision Evidence Accept replacement?\nfeasible An operating point independently satisfies the original AC equations, exact droops and bounds within the frozen tolerances Yes, if every required condition passes\ncertified_infeasible A replayable global necessary-condition contradiction No\nunresolved Neither a validated point nor a certificate No\n\nFeasibility does not require an optimal objective or a successful optimizer termination code. An iteration-limited result can contain a valid witness. Conversely, LOCALLY_INFEASIBLE, a failed multistart campaign, and a positive local Phase-I objective do not prove that no feasible operating point exists.","title":"Feasibility before droop replacement"},{"category":"section","location":"feasibility_gate/#Contract-and-independent-replay","page":"Feasibility qualification","text":"FeasibilityProblem copies and fingerprints the entire case: topology, availability, demand, generator limits, voltage and thermal limits, exact droop knots, fixed equipment settings, selected equipment bounds, optional branch angle limits and all validation tolerances. Changing any of these requires a new qualification. Mutation of a frozen snapshot raises an error. Solver settings and the search objective are recorded with attempts; they do not change the physical feasible set.\n\nwrite_feasibility_result(path, problem, result) saves the full contract and evidence. read_feasibility_result(path, problem) requires the expected contract, rejects stale scope, and replays the saved witness or certificate before reuse.\n\nTaps and shunts default to their fixed source settings. A witness must include the actual settings of selected equipment. Only selected banks may move; their optimization is continuous susceptance optimization, not discrete switching. The checker reconstructs those settings, then checks AC balance, both branch terminals, voltage, generator P/Q, unavailable generation, exact droop Q, controller P limits, reference angles and supplied branch angle limits.\n\nBranch does not currently store angle-difference limits. Supply source-case limits through angle_limits=Dict(branch_id => (min_radians,max_radians)) when they belong to the required problem. These limits are enforced in the local search as well as independent replay.","title":"Contract and independent replay"},{"category":"section","location":"feasibility_gate/#Usage","page":"Feasibility qualification","text":"qualification = qualify_droop_replacement(source_case, candidate_case;\n    calibration_state = source_opf_state,\n    load_factors = (1.0, 0.9),\n    initial_states = [source_opf_state, source_90_state],\n    witnesses = Dict(\"source/0.9\" => [FeasibilityWitness(source_90_state)]),\n    problem_options = (angle_limits = source_angle_limits,),\n)\nwrite_droop_qualification(\"qualification.json\", qualification)\naccepted_case = require_qualified_replacement(qualification)\n# Downstream independent-demand study analysis uses accepted_case; this line is unreachable on failure.\n\nThe calibration checks are replay-only: a local solve cannot hide a droop curve that misses the intended source point. Subsequent checks replay supplied points first, run analytical exclusion checks, and then optionally attempt a constant zero-objective local feasibility search. The search changes no droop parameters or unselected equipment. Set search_options=(solve_missing=false,) for an evidence-only replay.\n\nrequire_qualified_replacement rechecks every witness and fingerprint before returning a copy of the candidate. Do not bypass it in an acceptance workflow. Low-level case constructors and solve functions remain available for diagnostics and intentionally infeasible experiments; they are not acceptance APIs.\n\nTo qualify one individual replacement or a cumulative sequence, construct that candidate and call this same precursor at each step. Qualification of a previous candidate does not transfer to a changed set of curves.","title":"Usage"},{"category":"section","location":"feasibility_gate/#What-can-currently-be-certified-globally","page":"Feasibility qualification","text":"The built-in certificate path uses exact rational arithmetic on stored input numbers and includes the declared feasibility tolerances. It checks:\n\nActive generation capacity versus demand in every connected component. It ignores passive losses and shunt consumption, making this a necessary bound.\nReachable exact droop-Q intervals over allowed regulated-bus voltages.\nNodal reactive-power balance using these intervals, fixed/selected shunt bounds and the maximum reactive flow allowed by each terminal's thermal rating.\n\nThese are conservative outer bounds: a contradiction excludes all original feasible operating points. Certificates store their exact rational lower and upper bounds and are recomputed when reused. A solver's status is never imported as a certificate.\n\nThe default analytical path is incomplete. Optional global escalation adds a sparse outer LP with exact certificate replay, verified voltage-bound tightening, a sparse SOC relaxation, and bounded numerical global search with all droop regions. These backends are opt-in through lp_options and global_options; routine feasible-point replay does not launch them. An inconclusive backend or unsupported equipment scope remains unresolved. Solver-reported global bounds alone do not qualify as independently verified certificates.","title":"What can currently be certified globally"},{"category":"section","location":"feasibility_gate/#Independent-cases-versus-a-shared-security-policy","page":"Feasibility qualification","text":"A witness for one demand does not establish a common preventive dispatch across outages or a shared equipment setting across several demands. Check each witness against its declared contract, then use the corresponding coupled SCOPF model when shared/corrective policy is required.","title":"Independent cases versus a shared security policy"},{"category":"section","location":"feasibility_gate/#API","page":"Feasibility qualification","text":"The canonical docstrings are in the API reference:\n\nFeasibilityProblem\nFeasibilityWitness\nFeasibilityResult\nDroopQualification\nfeasibility_fingerprint\nvalidate_feasibility_witness\ncheck_feasibility\nverify_feasibility_result\nwrite_feasibility_result\nread_feasibility_result\nqualify_droop_replacement\nrequire_qualified_replacement\nwrite_droop_qualification","title":"API"},{"category":"section","location":"droop_optimization/#Choosing-a-shared-droop-design","page":"Droop parameter design","text":"Begin with a deliberately small, auditable problem: vary one positive volt-var slope over a bounded candidate set and solve the complete fixed-curve security-constrained AC OPF for every setting. This establishes the reference surface that in-model optimization must reproduce.\n\nBefore the reference API, read the curve tutorial and response versus design. Fixed-curve OPF finds the intersection of a supplied curve with the network. Parameter design also moves selected curves inside explicit bounds. Candidate slopes below use voltage-per-reactive-power units.\n\n(Image: Exact curve, deadband and saturation, illustrating the physical parameters selected by design.)\n\nFigure 1. A selected slope or reference changes the curve; voltage/output points remain constrained by AC balance. This illustration is not an optimized result.","title":"Choosing a shared droop design"},{"category":"section","location":"droop_optimization/#Contract","page":"Droop parameter design","text":"sweep_droop_slope(study, control_id, slopes):\n\npreserves the input study and changes only the selected control's slope;\nalways includes and solves the declared reference slope;\nsolves candidates outward from the reference and warm-starts from the nearest valid result;\nvalidates every candidate independently with equilibrium_report;\nrecords objective, worst voltage deviation, maximum branch loading, exact droop residual, validity, and solver termination status.\n\nThe slope uses the existing VoltVarDroop convention: per-unit voltage change per unit reactive output. Candidate and reference slopes must be positive and finite.","title":"Contract"},{"category":"section","location":"droop_optimization/#Example","page":"Droop parameter design","text":"sweep = sweep_droop_slope(\n    study,\n    2,\n    0.04:0.005:0.10;\n    reference_slope = 0.075,\n    smooth_epsilon = 1e-5,\n)\n\nbest = best_droop_slope(sweep)\nwrite_droop_slope_sweep(\"m3_slope_sweep.json\", sweep)\nwrite_droop_slope_sweep_plot(\"m3_slope_tradeoff.svg\", sweep)\n\nbest_droop_slope selects the valid sampled point with the smallest fixed-curve SCOPF dispatch objective. It is a parameter-search result, not a global optimality certificate. The AC model is nonconvex, and the current objective may be weakly sensitive to the droop setting, so voltage and loading diagnostics should be reviewed with the objective.","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"droop_optimization/#Fixed-curve-reproduction-check","page":"Droop parameter design","text":"When the selected slope equals the slope already stored in the study, the sweep reconstructs the same fixed-curve SCOPF problem. The test suite requires that reference point to reproduce the independently valid fixed-curve SCOPF objective. This prevents the droop design search layer from silently changing contingency policy or physical semantics.","title":"Fixed-curve reproduction check"},{"category":"section","location":"droop_optimization/#Bounded-in-model-design","page":"Droop parameter design","text":"optimize_droop_parameters promotes selected physical settings to variables shared across the base case and every training contingency. Bounds omitted by the caller fix that setting at its fixed-curve SCOPF value. Available settings are:\n\npositive slope;\npositive voltage reference;\nnonnegative lower and upper deadband widths.\n\nThe two widths may differ, providing an asymmetric deadband while preserving the standard saturated VoltVarDroop family. Both widths may also be fixed at zero while slope and reference remain free; this uses the specialized affine raw response described in Solvers. Variable-width bounds that permit collapse to zero remain rejected. The active/reactive dispatch objective remains the fixed-curve SCOPF objective; no hidden design penalty is introduced.\n\ndesign = optimize_droop_parameters(\n    study,\n    2;\n    slope_bounds = (0.04, 0.10),\n    v_ref_bounds = (0.995, 1.005),\n    deadband_low_bounds = (0.005, 0.015),\n    deadband_high_bounds = (0.005, 0.015),\n    smooth_epsilon = 1e-5,\n)\n\nwith_droop_settings(study, design) reconstructs an ordinary typed study. validate_droop_design(study, design) independently replays every state against that reconstructed exact PWL curve. A design is not accepted from solver status alone.","title":"Bounded in-model design"},{"category":"section","location":"droop_optimization/#Solver-scope","page":"Droop parameter design","text":"Bounded parameter design is a smooth nonlinear program supported by Ipopt and MadNLP. For the fixed-curve SCOPF/droop design regression fixture, MadNLP is warm-started from validated fixed-curve SCOPF states; an unseeded solve can select an infeasible local point. The two smooth solvers are required to agree on the validated objective, not on the parameter vector, because multiple settings can be nearly equivalent.\n\nThe single-controller optimize_droop_parameters(study, control_id; ...) overload rejects CCOpt.Optimizer. The vector DroopControl overload instead accepts encoding=:complementarity; see solver compatibility. Once a design is reconstructed as a fixed exact curve, CCOpt can independently re-solve it through solve_scopf(optimized; encoding=:complementarity). The solver-independent validate_droop_design exact replay remains the primary acceptance gate.","title":"Solver scope"},{"category":"section","location":"droop_optimization/#Held-out-scenarios","page":"Droop parameter design","text":"evaluate_held_out_contingencies holds the optimized base dispatch fixed and solves contingencies whose IDs were absent from training:\n\nheld_out = evaluate_held_out_contingencies(\n    study,\n    design,\n    [Contingency(:line_33; branch_ids = [33])],\n)\nheld_out.report.valid\n\nThe returned study, result, and report retain the normal fixed-curve SCOPF data and validation contracts. write_droop_design and read_droop_design provide a versioned JSON round trip for the optimized settings and training result.","title":"Held-out scenarios"},{"category":"section","location":"droop_optimization/#Visual-validation","page":"Droop parameter design","text":"write_droop_design_comparison_plot overlays the reference and optimized exact PWL curves with the optimized training operating points. Held-out study and result pairs may be supplied to add their non-base scenarios in a distinct colour:\n\nwrite_droop_design_comparison_plot(\n    \"m3_droop_design_comparison.svg\",\n    study,\n    design;\n    held_out_study = held_out.study,\n    held_out_result = held_out.result,\n)\n\nThe function intentionally covers only the domain-specific curve comparison. The workflow composes it with the existing fixed-curve SCOPF scenario plots rather than introducing a separate plotting framework or dependency.\n\nThe comparison distinguishes training points from the held-out line-33 point and explicitly records that the optimized G9 control has no operating point when G9 is unavailable. The held-out result is also passed through the normal fixed-curve SCOPF residual-to-tolerance visualization; the horizontal threshold at one remains the numerical acceptance boundary.","title":"Visual validation"},{"category":"section","location":"droop_optimization/#Validation-checks","page":"Droop parameter design","text":"Useful design checks include:\n\nfixed bounds at the reference reproduce the fixed-curve SCOPF objective;\nslope-only optimization agrees with the minimum of the fixed-slope sweep;\ngeneralized bounded settings satisfy every training scenario;\nreconstructed exact curves pass independent replay;\nat least one excluded contingency passes held-out evaluation;\nthe end-to-end workflow regenerates machine-readable and visual artifacts.","title":"Validation checks"},{"category":"section","location":"droop_optimization/#Scope-limit","page":"Droop parameter design","text":"droop design optimizes the identifiable physical parameters of the standard saturated volt-var family. Reactive capability and reactive output at deadband remain fixed. Arbitrary free-knot PWL topology optimization is intentionally deferred: without a separate regularization and identifiability contract it can overfit a small contingency set while producing non-unique, hard-to-interpret curves.","title":"Scope limit"},{"category":"section","location":"droop_optimization/#Multi-controller-security-design","page":"Droop parameter design","text":"The vector DroopControl interface and iterative contingency-addition driver are documented in Droop security design. They share the ordinary SCOPF builder and retain equipment policy equipment policies.","title":"Multi-controller security design"},{"category":"section","location":"scopf/#Security-constrained-AC-OPF","page":"SCOPF policies and API","text":"Security-constrained optimal power flow (SCOPF) couples one preventive operating point with a declared set of outage equilibria. Each state has its own AC voltages, angles and generator outputs. The study states which controls are shared and what may respond after an outage.\n\nStart with the complete security tutorial and the policy diagrams. The snippets here are API fragments; case, initial states, selected controls and their limits must be supplied by the caller. Notation and units defines the common symbols.\n\n(Image: Shared controls and distinct base/outage AC equilibria.)\n\nFigure 1. The arrows impose shared-parameter and response policies. They do not represent a chronological simulation of a disturbance.","title":"Security-constrained AC OPF"},{"category":"section","location":"scopf/#Active-response","page":"SCOPF policies and API","text":"All powers use the case system base. Let g index generators and c outage scenarios, with 0 denoting the base. In preventive mode,\n\nP_gc=operatornameclip(P_g0+alpha_gclambda_cL_gcU_gc)\n\nP_g0 and P_gc are base/outage active output in pu; alpha_gcge0 is a dimensionless normalized survivor weight; lambda_c is the common active-response request in pu; and L_gcU_gc are effective active bounds in pu. operatornameclip(zLU)=min(Umax(Lz)) for a real input z and lower/upper bounds LU.\n\nEffective bounds intersect generator and attached control capability with any declared movement limits. A saturated unit holds its bound while the common signal can increase to let remaining participants supply the required balance. Zero-weight survivors hold their base output. Unavailable generators inject zero. The angle-reference bus does not supply unbounded power.\n\nStudy defaults to default_participation(case): nonzero effective active-capability-range weights normalized among eligible survivors. Supply actual weights when known. An explicitly empty weight map selects no participants. Weights describe steady-state sharing, not governor speed or frequency dynamics. The result's balancing_power field stores the common request signal; after clipping it need not equal the total delivered output change.\n\nCorrective active redispatch replaces the response equation with\n\n-r_gle P_gc-P_g0le r_g\n\nHere r_g is the finite nonnegative symmetric movement allowance in pu from redispatch_limits[g]. Omitted units have zero permitted movement. This allowance is not a ramp rate; participation weights are rejected in corrective mode.","title":"Active response"},{"category":"section","location":"scopf/#Reactive-controls-and-capability","page":"SCOPF policies and API","text":"Surviving devices retain their supplied reactive modes, references and measured locations. Their reactive outputs respond to each scenario's AC solution. Unavailable generators have zero P/Q and no active controller equation. An unavailable monitored branch disables its branch-terminal regulator.\n\nGenerator/control P/Q bounds remain active. An explicitly supplied apparent rating additionally restricts capability as described in mixed controls. A fixed curve is not retuned per outage.","title":"Reactive controls and capability"},{"category":"section","location":"scopf/#Equipment-timing-and-movement-bounds","page":"SCOPF policies and API","text":"Only selected equipment may be optimized. Declare its timing explicitly:\n\npolicy = SCOPFControlPolicy(\n    taps=:preventive,\n    shunts=:corrective,\n    reactive=:automatic,\n    shunt_movement_limits=Dict(201 => 0.02))\n\nstudy = Study(case;\n    contingencies=contingencies,\n    participation=default_participation(case),\n    tap_controls=[TapControl(11; lower=0.95, upper=1.05)],\n    shunt_controls=[ShuntControl(201)],\n    control_policy=policy)\n\nThis fragment assumes a transformer ID 11 and a supported simple bank ID 201. The tap ratio bounds are dimensionless; the bank movement limit is 0.02 pu susceptance. A preventive setting is shared by all states where the device is available. A corrective setting may differ by scenario inside absolute bounds and any declared base-to-outage movement limit. Unselected devices stay fixed.\n\nSelected taps/shunts default to corrective timing if it is omitted. A movement map of nothing adds no extra movement cap beyond absolute control bounds. A supplied map defines the allowed-action set: selected devices omitted from that map are frozen. An empty map therefore permits no selected corrective movement. Outaging the selected device removes its state/coupling equation; it is not a movement action.\n\nThese controls are continuous steady-state relaxations. Timing permission does not supply a response delay, legal discrete position, switching count or a verified transition trajectory. Immediate/settled assessment functions compare separately declared stages under the same base lineage; they do not simulate the movement between those stages.","title":"Equipment timing and movement bounds"},{"category":"section","location":"scopf/#Objective","page":"SCOPF policies and API","text":"The default objective penalizes the base schedule's deviation from supplied reference outputs:\n\nJ=sum_ginmathcal Gleft(P_g0-P_g^initial)^2\n    +10^-3(Q_g0-Q_g^initial)^2right\n\nmathcal G is the generator set, Q_g0 base reactive output and the superscript initial denotes supplied reference outputs. J has weighted squared pu units, with no monetary units. Outage states impose feasibility constraints, so their recourse can be nonunique. With no outages, this reduces to the corresponding base OPF.","title":"Objective"},{"category":"section","location":"scopf/#Solve-and-independently-check","page":"SCOPF policies and API","text":"study = Study(case;\n    contingencies=[Contingency(:line_22; branch_ids=[22]),\n                   Contingency(:generator_9; generator_ids=[9])],\n    participation=default_participation(case))\nresult = solve_scopf(study; smooth_epsilon=1e-5)\nreport = equilibrium_report(study, result)\n\nThis fragment requires those device IDs in case. Check the report and native solver status; inspect each scenario rather than only the base. To assess an already chosen base state, use evaluate_contingencies(study, base_state). That path freezes its base; it does not independently choose a new preventive schedule for each outage. See validation for exact replay.\n\nFor storage/reuse, write_study and write_scopf_result preserve the declared input and result contracts. Reload and revalidate against the expected study; a saved success flag alone is insufficient. Historical response contracts are not silently migrated to a different policy.","title":"Solve and independently check"},{"category":"section","location":"scopf/#Catalogue,-ranking-and-complete-coverage","page":"SCOPF policies and API","text":"contingency_catalog(case) enumerates available line, transformer and generator outages with stable IDs. Inspect eligible_contingencies and excluded_contingencies before interpreting coverage. The joint formulation requires a connected base and does not balance arbitrary islands. A separate evaluate_islanding_contingency assesses a retained component and reports removed demand and capacity; discarded demand is not classified as served.\n\nevaluate_contingency_catalog evaluates the eligible catalogue from one validated base. contingency_screening_scores prioritizes work without certifying safety. The optional filtering pipeline reports routing_complete, full_ac_coverage and all_outcomes_accepted separately. A screened-but-unsolved outage is not an accepted AC result. Unsupported or uncertain predictions must fall back to exact assessment; final required coverage needs actual AC replay.\n\nrun_contingency_campaign supports input-fingerprinted checkpoints. Changed network data, base states, policies, numerical settings or solver versions need a new output directory. Checkpoint reuse revalidates saved states; errors and incomplete records remain visible. Ranking, a bounded subset or a numerical failure does not prove full security or physical infeasibility.\n\nFor a shared curve design with iterative scenario addition, read droop security design. For chronological coupling, read multi-period design.","title":"Catalogue, ranking and complete coverage"},{"category":"section","location":"release_scope/#Supported-scope-and-limitations","page":"Supported scope","text":"DroopOPF solves balanced, positive-sequence steady-state AC models with explicit reactive controls and declared operating/control constraints.\n\nCapability Supported model Interpretation limit\nBase OPF AC balance, voltage bounds, generator capability and both-terminal branch limits Local nonlinear solutions require independent validation.\nReactive control FreeQ, FixedQ, AVR and saturated/deadband Volt/VAR curves These are equilibrium relations, not dynamic controller models.\nEquipment design Selected continuous transformer ratios and supported simple-bank admittances Legal discrete positions and device movement trajectories need separate checks.\nSecurity Coupled base/outage states, limit-aware response and declared equipment recourse Acceptance applies to the stated catalogue; exclusions and unresolved solves remain explicit.\nShared curve design Bounded slopes, references and supported deadband widths Multiple local or weakly identified designs can exist.\nMulti-period operation Shared controls, period-specific dispatch, durations and declared ramps No inferred storage, commitment or dynamic transition model.\nPSS/E import RAW 33/34/36 parsing, strict supported AC conversion and saved-state replay Native version-specific export evidence and missing ratings must be reported explicitly.\nFeasibility qualification Physical witnesses and supported independently checked outer certificates A timeout or failed local solve is not an infeasibility proof.\n\nThe base OPF objective is weighted schedule deviation. The base multi-period objective is active loss energy. Optional reserve-aware policies are documented separately; no generator fuel costs or manufacturer limits are invented.\n\nAlways declare system bases, ratings, generator availability, reactive modes, control bounds, equipment timing and movement budgets before comparing results. Use validation to check the returned physical state and policy. The API reference documents individual entry points.","title":"Supported scope and limitations"},{"category":"section","location":"development/#Development","page":"Development","text":"","title":"Development"},{"category":"section","location":"development/#Local-checks","page":"Development","text":"Run the package tests from the repository root:\n\nJULIA_NUM_THREADS=2 OPENBLAS_NUM_THREADS=1 julia --project=. -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.test()'\n\nBuild the documentation locally:\n\njulia --project=docs -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.develop(path = \".\"); Pkg.instantiate()'\njulia --project=docs docs/make.jl\n\nThe generated site is in docs/build/. Two Julia threads exercise the bounded contingency worker tests; serial-only runs skip that concurrency branch.","title":"Local checks"},{"category":"section","location":"development/#Continuous-integration-and-deployment","page":"Development","text":".github/workflows/CI.yml runs the package tests on Julia 1.10 and 1.12. .github/workflows/Documentation.yml builds the docs for pull requests and deploys the main documentation to GitHub Pages through Documenter.jl.\n\nThe deployment workflow requires GitHub Pages to be enabled for the repository. Documenter uses the repository GITHUB_TOKEN, so no deploy key is needed for this same-repository deployment.","title":"Continuous integration and deployment"},{"category":"section","location":"development/#Maintain-the-teaching-documentation","page":"Development","text":"Teaching pages introduce the electrical problem before the API and define each symbol locally, with the common notation reference as an index. Use complete executed @example blocks for runnable tutorials; label partial reference snippets as fragments. Give diagrams descriptive alternative text and explain their assumptions in captions. Explain the assumptions of each illustrative calculation.\n\nFrom the repository root, regenerate the nine deterministic SVG schematics and check the rendered documentation:\n\npython3 tools/docs/build_teaching_figures.py\njulia --project=docs docs/make.jl\npython3 tools/docs/check_teaching_docs.py docs/build\n\nThe docs environment must first be instantiated with the local package developed into it. Its existing project/manifest describes the build dependencies. Review the built pages visually, including curves, circuit signs and equation tables. The link checker checks local targets/anchors and teaching-figure metadata; the Documenter build executes tutorial assertions. Neither substitutes for reviewing the physical meaning of a formula.","title":"Maintain the teaching documentation"},{"category":"section","location":"droop_security/#Droop-design-with-contingency-addition","page":"Design with contingency addition","text":"","title":"Droop design with contingency addition"},{"category":"section","location":"droop_security/#Physical-and-acceptance-contract","page":"Design with contingency addition","text":"The design variables are selected controllers' positive slopes (pu voltage per pu reactive power), voltage references (pu), and nonnegative lower/upper deadband widths (pu). One setting vector is shared by the base and every training outage. Reactive capability and deadband output remain supplied data. The existing saturated Volt–VAr law, AC balance equations, operating limits and base schedule-deviation objective are unchanged. Each scenario has independent AC states; an outaged generator has zero P/Q and no active controller equation. Active response follows the Study participation/redispatch policy. Selected taps and shunts retain their preventive/corrective bounds and movement budgets.\n\nThe default smooth design NLP uses the existing SCOPF builder. Pass encoding=:complementarity to optimize_droop_parameters(study, controls; ...) for a CCOpt design with the exact deadband and saturation graph. Omit optimizer_factory in exact mode and supply CCOpt options through optimizer_attributes. Slopes, references and declared deadband bounds remain shared across scenarios in either encoding. Exact shared-parameter design currently uses the case's attached droop controls; explicit reactive_assignments remain supported by the smooth design path and are rejected by this exact builder. Independent validation reconstructs exact physical curves and checks the design bounds, AC physics, outage availability and inter-scenario coupling. Fixed design bounds must reproduce fixed-curve SCOPF; no contingencies must reproduce base-case design. Multiple local optima may exist: objective agreement is an experimental check, not a global-optimality assertion.\n\nThe small exact shared-design regression in test/test_droop_security.jl uses an initial barrier of 1e-6, a 1e-14 proportional relaxation floor, native tolerance 1e-11 and zero bound relaxation. Its two-controller, one-generator-outage fixture has nonzero fixed deadbands and passes the unchanged independent physical gate. These are recorded numerical settings; the test does not establish convergence from arbitrary starts or at 500 buses.\n\nContingency addition starts from a declared subset, solves a shared design, recomputes screening at the new base/settings, assesses required outages, and adds unresolved omitted outages. Solver rejection is unresolved evidence, not proven infeasibility. Screening alone never permits successful termination: every eligible outage receives a full AC audit at the final design. Islanding entries remain explicit exclusions from the joint design and prevent a full network N-1 claim. Iteration limits, failed masters and unresolved training outages remain unsuccessful outcomes.","title":"Physical and acceptance contract"},{"category":"section","location":"droop_security/#Read-the-shared-design","page":"Design with contingency addition","text":"(Image: Shared control parameters and distinct scenario equilibria.)\n\nFigure 1. Training scenarios share a curve parameter vector. Final omitted outages must be checked again at the retained design and frozen base policy.\n\nThe Volt/VAR tutorial derives the exact law. In this page's notation, j indexes controllers, c scenarios (0 is base), and g generators. Q_jc is the attached generator's output; V_jc its measured voltage; Q_j^0 the supplied deadband output; Q_j^minQ_j^max the Q interval shown in the fixed-box formula. z_+=max(z0) and operatornameclip(zLU)=min(Umax(Lz)). Here z is a real input and LU lower/upper bounds in the same units. Rated generators instead use their effective P-dependent limits. For controller j, with slope m_j, reference v_j and nonnegative half-widths d_j^- and d_j^+, the independently replayed response is\n\nQ_jc=operatornameclipleft(Q_j^0+\nfracv_j-d_j^- - V_jc_+m_j-\nfracV_jc-v_j-d_j^+_+m_j Q_j^minQ_j^maxright)\n\nAll design parameters are shared over c. In the objective below, P_g0Q_g0 are base outputs and superscript initial denotes supplied reference outputs; the objective has weighted squared pu units. It remains\n\nmin sum_g left(P_g0-P_g^mathrminitial)^2\n+10^-3(Q_g0-Q_g^mathrminitial)^2right\n\nFor preventive active response, surviving generators obey P[g,c] = clip(P[g,0] + alpha[g,c]*signal[c], L[g,c], U[g,c]), with capability-aware weights renormalised over survivors and saturation allowing remaining participants to take over. Zero-weight units hold base output. Corrective mode instead enforces the declared redispatch bounds. The final audit holds the complete base AC state and base equipment settings fixed; it never reoptimises a different base separately for each outage.","title":"Read the shared design"},{"category":"section","location":"droop_security/#API-and-reproducible-runs","page":"Design with contingency addition","text":"catalog = contingency_catalog(case)\nbase_study = Study(case; participation=participation)\ncontrols = [DroopControl(1; slope_bounds=(0.04,0.08)),\n            DroopControl(2; slope_bounds=(0.04,0.10))]\nresult = optimize_droop_security(base_study, controls;\n    catalog, max_rounds=5, batch_size=1,\n    initial_states=Dict(:base=>validated_base),\n    solver_kwargs=(;smooth_epsilon=1e-5,\n        optimizer_attributes=Dict(\"max_iter\"=>1000,\"max_cpu_time\"=>30.0)))\nwrite_droop_security(\"security.json\", result)\n\nUse optimize_droop_parameters(study, controls) for the corresponding full training-set design. validate_droop_design(study, design) returns valid, policy_valid, and the ordinary independent report. The scalar droop design interface remains available and now preserves equipment policy during reconstruction and held-out evaluation. Attached legacy droops and explicit reactive assignments cannot be combined in this design interface; unsupported combinations reject rather than silently dropping a controller policy.\n\nDroopSecurityResult.eligible_accepted covers the supplied eligible catalog. full_n1_accepted additionally requires the complete network catalog and no excluded islands. A successful result contains a final exhaustive audit; a failed master contains its validation outcome and never receives an inferred security pass. on_iteration can checkpoint the entire round after assessment. Independent assessment errors are retained, and added IDs use deterministic error/violation ordering. The algorithm adds scenario constraints, not linear cuts or a decomposition with convergence/global-optimality guarantees.\n\nsparse_balance=true opts smooth OPF/SCOPF into omitting exact-zero admittance terms even with droop controls. It changes expression sparsity, not equations. Legacy controlled-case defaults remain unchanged because solver trajectories can be sensitive to numerical representation. The public runner declares this option and bounded solver settings in its protocol. Time limits apply during optimization, not package loading or model construction.\n\nOptional plotting needs Matplotlib. Exhaustive assessments use input-fingerprinted checkpoints; a changed case, source, policy or solver budget requires a new directory. The subsequent design trace is retained round-by-round but is not automatically resumable. Fresh design runs must use fresh evidence directories if prior results are to be preserved.\n\nTo assess only branch contingencies (lines and transformers), retain the original runs and use separate directories:\n\nThe branch-only catalog retains islanding branch outages in the exhaustive assessment and excludes generator outages entirely. The joint design still handles only connected outages. A successful eligible branch audit therefore does not imply full-network N-1 acceptance. retained/ saves the valid master with the largest training set, then most independently accepted outages, then lowest objective. Its acceptance manifest lists the exact coverage; a later rejected master never replaces this candidate with an invalid design. optimized-study.json and master-result.json use the public read_study and read_scopf_result interfaces. The replay example verifies their round trip, independently revalidates every saved accepted outage, and checks that all witnesses use the identical retained base state. The comparison report also checks that the original evidence files have not changed against the saved verification/pre-branch-preservation.json manifest (run it from the repository root). The optional --prior replay exports accepted branch witnesses from an earlier fixed-base assessment separately. It verifies matching study/base inputs and revalidates the saved states; it does not re-solve them or count them as new successful attempts.","title":"API and reproducible runs"},{"category":"section","location":"droop_security/#Interpret-the-coverage","page":"Design with contingency addition","text":"A retained candidate must pass native solver acceptance and independent AC, control and policy checks over the declared final catalogue. Report exclusions, failed solves and omitted outages explicitly. An unresolved numerical solve is not proof that the network is infeasible. A ranked or screened subset does not establish complete outage security.","title":"Interpret the coverage"},{"category":"section","location":"psse/#PSS/E-RAW-data-parser","page":"PSS/E import and saved solutions","text":"parse_psse_raw reads full-case bus/branch RAW revisions 33, 34 and 36. import_psse_case converts the supported electrical subset into a DroopOPF Case, with source identities, supplied operating points and diagnostics. load_psse_case returns just the converted case.\n\nraw = parse_psse_raw(\"network.raw\")\nraw.revision\nraw.sections[:generator][1].fields\n\n# Strict conversion: zero/omitted ratings require an explicit decision.\nimported = import_psse_case(raw; unrated_limit=10.0) # pu, only if intended\ncase = imported.case\nsupplied_state = imported.initial_state\nidentities = imported.source_ids\nmessages = imported.diagnostics\n\nThere are no added package dependencies or PSS/E installation requirements. This parser does not read binary .sav, .dyr, .seq or JSON .rawx files. IC=1 change-case files are rejected because they need an existing network. No optimization is launched by parsing or importing.","title":"PSS/E RAW data parser"},{"category":"section","location":"psse/#Version-handling","page":"PSS/E import and saved solutions","text":"Feature v33 v34 v36\nBus fields and supplied Vm/Va Parsed Parsed Parsed\nLoad fields Original layout Distributed-generation fields Text load type and name\nFixed shunts Original layout Same layout Additional name\nGenerator fields Original layout NREG at tail NREG before MBASE, BASLOD, droop-controller name and machine name\nBranch ratings Three ratings Name and twelve ratings Twelve ratings plus bypass flag\nSwitched shunts N/B block pairs NREG extension ID, NREG, name and S/N/B block triples\nTransformer records Four or five rows Expanded winding ratings Expanded winding ratings\nAdditional sections Retained System-wide/switching/substation data retained Voltage droop, switching-rating tables, load types and interfaces retained\n\nThe parser handles comma or whitespace delimiters, single/double quoted fields, doubled quote escapes, commas/slashes inside names, trailing comments, @! headers, empty fields, CRLF, UTF-8 BOM and Fortran D exponents. Optional omitted fields remain absent in .fields; conversion applies documented defaults. Use encoding=:latin1 for legacy extended-ASCII text.\n\nEach PSSERecord retains typed known fields, original token rows and line numbers. PSSERawData.text retains the source text; unknown fields and sections are preserved. Transformer regulation tails are retained as control_fields_1, control_fields_2 and control_fields_3 rather than guessing minor-version node/control-field ordering. Extended bus-name references are retained by the parser; current AC conversion requires numeric bus references.\n\nDelimiter comments determine section transitions when present. Bare zero terminators use the declared revision's section order. Substation internals are opaque, preserving nested records and zero delimiters. Malformed numeric fields, unterminated quotes, truncated transformers, unsupported revisions, premature EOF and data after Q produce source/line errors.","title":"Version handling"},{"category":"section","location":"psse/#Electrical-conversion","page":"PSS/E import and saved solutions","text":"The strict AC subset includes energized buses; in-service constant-power loads; fixed shunts; generators (including equal minimum/maximum Q); lines; fixed 2-winding transformer ratios/phases; and switched shunts locked at supplied BINIT. Load impedance components are represented exactly as voltage-squared fixed shunts. Constant-current loads and embedded distributed-generation load models are rejected. Unavailable generators, branches and shunts retain their availability flags. Type-4 buses and their attached equipment are excluded from the energized case, with diagnostics; their original records remain available.\n\nMW/MVAr values are divided by SBASE; angles are converted from degrees to radians. Fixed shunt BL and switched-shunt BINIT retain the positive-capacitive sign. Generator identity is (bus, ID), and branch identity includes (I,J,CKT); integer domain IDs are mapped back through .source_ids.\n\nTransformer CW=1/2/3 and CZ=1/2/3 conversions account for system MVA base, nominal winding kV, winding-bus kV, secondary winding ratio and phase. Tests compare all nine combinations against an independent two-port admittance primitive. Magnetizing admittance and impedance-correction tables are rejected; they are not silently moved to fixed bus shunts that would lose transformer outage association. Nonzero branch-end shunts and bypassed branches are also rejected by conversion.\n\nZero/omitted thermal ratings are not assigned a fabricated finite limit. Conversion requires an explicit unrated_limit in pu and records its use. The source XFRRAT/NXFRAT current-rating unit options are retained, but conversion currently requires MVA ratings. It uses the first normal rating; remaining ratings remain available in the parsed records.\n\nThere must be exactly one energized reference bus for AC conversion. Supplied angles are shifted by its angle. .initial_state retains supplied generator P/Q, including values outside reported limits; only generator optimizer start hints are clamped, with diagnostics. Import success does not certify the source operating point's feasibility.\n\nAutomatic PSS/E voltage regulation, remote control, tap/shunt switching and voltage-droop controllers are not inferred as library droop/AVR controls. Cases with nonempty voltage-droop, DC, FACTS, GNE, induction-machine, switching-device, interface, substation or other unsupported electrical sections are rejected by conversion. Three-winding transformers parse as five-row records but are not yet converted to star equivalents. These records remain accessible in raw.","title":"Electrical conversion"},{"category":"section","location":"psse/#Public-data-and-qualification-limits","page":"PSS/E import and saved solutions","text":"Existing public ARPA-E 14- and 500-bus v33 RAW files are exercised without modification.\nA genuine PSS/E 34.6 IEEE 14-bus file is included unchanged under test/data/psse/public_v34_ieee14.raw, with pinned URL, SHA-256 and the upstream MPL-2.0 license in the same directory. Source: VeraGrid fixture.\nA public v33 synthetic South Carolina 500-bus RAW was tested: 500 buses, 90 generators, 466 lines and 131 transformers. Source: PowSyBl public fixture.\nSynthetic v33/v34/v36 fixtures describe the same electrical circuit and test version-specific field positions. No native public v36 exporter file was found in the searched sources. Its present qualification is schema-based; this is not full PSS/E 36 exporter/interoperability certification.\n\nThe implementation references the versioned VeraGrid RAW schema definitions and independently checks transformer conversions against PowerModels' converter equations. V36 additions were cross-checked with the Siemens-authored PSS/E 36.1 Data Formats Reference Manual, sections 1.8–1.13 and RAW format change summary (publicly indexed manual copy); the proprietary manual is not redistributed. In particular, v36 BP and the new sections must not be inferred solely from an inherited v35 branch schema.\n\nParsing and saved-state replay do not themselves qualify a new control policy or an outage-security study.","title":"Public data and qualification limits"},{"category":"section","location":"psse/#Check-a-new-file-against-its-saved-power-flow-state","page":"PSS/E import and saved solutions","text":"The ordinary loader now checks the RAW saved solution automatically and logs a compact result. It retains its Case return type. A failed saved-state check warns by default; require_valid=true makes it an acceptance gate:\n\ncase = load_psse_case(\"network.raw\";\n    report_path=\"network.validation.json\", require_valid=true)\n\nThe strict case loader still requires an explicit unrated_limit for missing ratings. To inspect a file without constructing a case for optimization, use the report-only check. This also works when thermal ratings are absent:\n\nreport = check_psse_file(\"network.raw\";\n    report_path=\"network.validation.json\")\nreport[\"power_flow_consistent\"]\nreport[\"known_limits_valid\"]\nreport[\"thermal_coverage_complete\"]\nreport[\"worst_buses\"]\n\nOn the work machine, from the repository directory:\n\njulia --project=. examples/check_psse_file.jl network.raw network.validation.json\n\nExport the solved case from PSS/E to RAW, retaining bus VM/VA and generator PG/QG. The check never re-solves, redispatches, adjusts equipment, clamps the saved state or uploads data. It recomputes both-terminal branch flows, branch losses, shunt consumption and P/Q balance at every bus using the saved values. The JSON report includes source file SHA-256, revision, tolerances, bus mismatches, branch flows, source identities and conversion diagnostics. It refuses to overwrite the source RAW file, including an existing symlink alias.\n\nInterpret the results separately:\n\nStatus Meaning\npassed Saved state satisfies AC balance and the assessed supported normal limits\npower_flow_mismatch At least one bus exceeds the declared P/Q balance tolerance\nlimits_violated AC balance passes but a supported operating limit fails\nlimits_unassessed Balance and known limits pass, but active branches lack ratings\nmissing_saved_solution Required VM/VA or PG/QG fields are absent; defaults cannot certify a saved solution\nunsupported An electrical model cannot be faithfully converted for this check\ninvalid_file File cannot be read or parsed\n\nThe CLI exits 0 only for passed, and 2 for a reported failure or incomplete assessment. Invalid command options or report-write errors remain errors. A solved PSS/E case can balance correctly while violating generator or thermal limits; power_flow_consistent identifies this distinction even when overall valid is false.\n\nDefault balance thresholds are 0.01 MW and 0.01 MVAr per bus, explicitly reported to accommodate common RAW output rounding. They are configurable:\n\nreport = check_psse_file(\"network.raw\";\n    power_tolerance_mw=0.001, reactive_tolerance_mvar=0.001,\n    limit_tolerance_pu=1e-6, top_buses=10)\n\nThese are saved-file comparison thresholds; the library's existing physical solver-acceptance tolerances are unchanged. Loader options use validation_options=(power_tolerance_mw=0.001, reactive_tolerance_mvar=0.001). Automatic checking can be disabled explicitly with check_saved_solution=false.\n\nFor report-only replay, an internal adapter marker lets admittances be evaluated without thermal ratings. That marker is never exposed as a source rating or used to accept thermal loading. Missing ratings appear as JSON null and remain unassessed. No case with such a marker is returned by check_psse_file.\n\nThis checks the supported AC equations at the state saved in RAW. RAW does not supply an independent branch-flow result listing, so recomputed flows are not claimed to have been compared against a separate PSS/E branch-flow export. Original PSS/E solver termination, remote/automatic control semantics, dynamics and security qualification remain outside this small check.","title":"Check a new file against its saved power-flow state"},{"category":"section","location":"robustness/#Robustness-and-diagnostics","page":"Robustness and repeated starts","text":"Use repeated starts to test whether a nonconvex solve returns comparable, independently valid operating points. Diagnose control breakpoints and binding limits separately from objective agreement.","title":"Robustness and diagnostics"},{"category":"section","location":"robustness/#Multi-start-comparison","page":"Robustness and repeated starts","text":"solve_scopf_multistart accepts a dictionary from a start name to the usual scenario/state dictionary. An empty dictionary is a cold start. Every run is solved and independently validated; invalid or divergent outcomes are retained.\n\nstarts = Dict(:cold => Dict(), :warm => previous_result.states)\ncomparison = solve_scopf_multistart(study, starts;\n    smooth_epsilon=1e-5, objective_atol=1e-8, objective_rtol=1e-6)\n\nThe classification is one of:\n\n:comparable: at least two independently valid objectives agree within atol + rtol * maximum(abs, objectives);\n:divergent: at least two valid objectives exceed that spread;\n:insufficient_valid_runs: exactly one run is independently valid;\n:no_valid_runs: no run passes validation with a finite objective.\n\nbest_run is selected only among independently valid runs. It is a convenience for follow-on analysis, not evidence that divergent local solutions are interchangeable. The JSON artifact records every labelled solver result, validation report, valid-run set, objective range, and decision.","title":"Multi-start comparison"},{"category":"section","location":"robustness/#Breakpoint-and-binding-diagnostics","page":"Robustness and repeated starts","text":"scopf_diagnostics(study, result) recomputes validation and returns a separate SCOPFDiagnostics artifact. It is returned separately from the stable SCOPFReport schema.\n\nFor each active generator control and scenario it records the regulated voltage, exact PWL regime, nearest breakpoint value and index, absolute voltage distance, and near-breakpoint classification. Structured findings cover solver failure, independent-validation failure, near-breakpoint operation, and bus, branch, or generator limits within binding_tolerance.\n\nThe critical contingency is the non-base scenario with the smallest independently recomputed margin across voltage, generator P/Q, and branch thermal limits. This minimum-margin rule is a screening heuristic; it is not a transient or voltage-stability ranking.","title":"Breakpoint and binding diagnostics"},{"category":"section","location":"robustness/#droop-design-design-parameter-starts","page":"Robustness and repeated starts","text":"optimize_droop_multistart varies actual starting values for slope, voltage reference, and both deadband widths inside the design NLP. Every named input, optimized design, and exact-curve validation report is retained. The result classifies objective agreement and separately reports component-wise final parameter spread, since similar objective values do not prove that the design parameters are identifiable.\n\nThe workflow writes design_multistart.json and design_multistart.md.","title":"droop design design-parameter starts"},{"category":"section","location":"robustness/#Record-performance-reproducibly","page":"Robustness and repeated starts","text":"benchmark_scopf records package/solver identity, model dimensions, elapsed time, Julia allocations, objective, termination and validation. Measure compilation, model construction, optimization and replay separately where possible. Process peak memory is a lifetime high-water mark; it is not incremental model memory.\n\nKeep all failed starts. Similar objective values can conceal different controller settings, and neither multi-start agreement nor a small test case proves global optimality or large-network scalability.","title":"Record performance reproducibly"},{"category":"section","location":"global_feasibility/#Optional-global-feasibility-escalation","page":"Global certificates","text":"A second-order cone (SOC) models a Euclidean-norm inequality; positive semidefinite (PSD) constraints model nonnegative quadratic forms. SOS2 means special ordered set of type 2: at most two adjacent interpolation weights may be nonzero, allowing an exact piecewise-linear (PWL) graph. Symbols for the certificate and lifted-voltage equations are defined below.\n\nGlobal analysis extends the existing three-outcome gate. It does not replace it. Only an independently checked AC point produces feasible; a replayable certificate produces certified_infeasible; all other outcomes are unresolved.\n\nThe scalable policy is to replay known points first, attempt local feasibility, then escalate selected unresolved cases. Do not make full global search a mandatory cost for every candidate, demand condition or contingency.","title":"Optional global feasibility escalation"},{"category":"section","location":"global_feasibility/#Implemented-layers","page":"Global certificates","text":"Layer Model/evidence Can establish feasibility? Can certify infeasibility?\nExisting gate Original AC/PWL replay and analytical bounds Yes Yes, for supported analytical contradictions\nSparse outer LP Voltage-square envelopes, edge-product SOC support cuts, terminal thermal cuts, full droop convex hull No Yes, with exact rational Farkas replay\nCycle PSD cuts Bounded-size matrix blocks with shared fill products and exact rational support cuts No Yes, through the same rational LP certificate checker\nLP bound tightening Min/max selected voltages, exact weak-duality bounds, rebuilt square/droop envelopes No Yes, if the refined LP yields a replayable contradiction\nSparse SOC relaxation Edge products, voltage-square envelopes, full droop convex hull No Numerical evidence only in this implementation\nGlobal search Rectangular AC equations, lifted edge flows, exact SOS2 droop regions Yes, after original-model replay Numerical solver evidence only; complete search-tree proof verification is not implemented\n\nAll models include generator availability, the study P/Q bounds, fixed source taps/shunts, both branch-terminal ratings and the frozen feasibility tolerances. Selected/free equipment is explicitly unsupported by these global backends; the gate records that limitation rather than freezing it silently. The verified LP currently requires zero branch phase shifts, which both imported test cases satisfy. It deliberately omits angle windows and rank-one constraints, so its feasible set contains the original feasible set. Optional cycle PSD cuts strengthen consistency between edges while retaining a valid outer relaxation.\n\nThe SOC/global models include supplied edge angle windows within (-pi/2,pi/2). Other windows are recorded as omitted. Edge angles are periodic, and one gauge is chosen per island; original unwrapped angles and reference requirements are always rechecked on returned points. Consequently these restrictions can be an outer representation of the original angle contract. They cannot exclude an original feasible point by silently selecting a droop region.\n\nGlobal search uses thermal-current bounds and shortest-path angle bounds to narrow rectangular voltage coordinates, with numerical outward padding. These numerical bounds are not themselves accepted as exact certificates.","title":"Implemented layers"},{"category":"section","location":"global_feasibility/#Symbols-and-geometric-meaning","page":"Global certificates","text":"A linear program (LP) optimizes a linear function under linear constraints. This outer LP contains every original feasible AC point; excluding the outer set therefore excludes the original set. The reverse implication does not hold. In the certificate below, x is an LP decision vector, A its inequality coefficient matrix, b its right-hand-side vector, lu finite componentwise bounds, y a nonnegative multiplier vector, and i a vector-coordinate index. A^mathsf T denotes transpose. Certificate margin m is a scalar, unrelated to the droop slope also written m on control pages.\n\nIn the voltage lift later below, V is the complex bus-voltage vector, W=VV^* its lifted outer-product matrix, * conjugate transpose, and z a complex test vector. Positive semidefinite (PSD) means z^*Wzge0 for every z. This z is not branch impedance. The LP's real decision vector contains real and imaginary components of such voltage-product quantities.","title":"Symbols and geometric meaning"},{"category":"section","location":"global_feasibility/#What-makes-an-LP-certificate-independently-checkable","page":"Global certificates","text":"For the outer LP Ax <= b, with finite box bounds l <= x <= u, the LP solver suggests nonnegative multipliers y. The checker computes, using exact rational arithmetic on stored inputs,\n\nm = sum_i min(A^mathsfTy)_i l_i\n                        (A^mathsfTy)_i u_i-b^mathsfTy\n\nIf m > 0, every point in the box contradicts the weighted inequalities. This remains valid when the numerical multiplier vector has a stationarity residual: the box correction accounts for it instead of assuming it is zero.\n\nNetwork admittances for zero phase shifts are constructed rationally from stored R, X, charging and tap data. Physical PWL knots are retained. SOC and thermal support directions are checked to have norm at most one. Floating-point LP rows are rounded outwards over their finite boxes. A certificate reader reconstructs the mathematical relaxation and verifies its positive rational contradiction; it does not trust HiGHS's infeasibility status.\n\nVoltage-bound tightening uses the corresponding weak-duality formula with the objective coefficient included. Each improved bound has its own multiplier proof. Bounds are rounded outwards to a dyadic grid to control arithmetic size. Every proof is replayed before rebuilding the droop graph's convex hull on a narrower domain. The ledger's displayed floating-point bounds are not evidence.\n\nverified_feasibility_voltage_bounds can supply these bounds to a subsequent global search. They apply to the original strict problem: passing them to a model that relaxes bus voltage is rejected. Use max_relaxation=0 for that search.","title":"What makes an LP certificate independently checkable"},{"category":"section","location":"global_feasibility/#Bounded-cycle-strengthening","page":"Global certificates","text":"Pass psd_clique_limit=8 to certify_feasibility_lp to enable cycle strengthening. The default remains zero for compatibility with earlier ledgers. A deterministic minimum-degree elimination builds small cliques and shared auxiliary voltage products for fill edges. Elimination stops if the next clique would exceed the limit (supported limits: 3–32). Any remaining dense core is left relaxed. Parallel circuits share their bus-voltage products, with conjugation for reversed edges.\n\nFor a physical voltage vector, the lifted matrix is W = V V*, so every principal block is positive semidefinite. At an LP solution, negative eigenvectors suggest cuts of the form z* W z >= 0. The vector is quantized to rational components; every such vector gives a valid cut, whether or not it is an accurate eigenvector. Replay reconstructs its exact rational coefficients and the clique from the network. Numerical eigenvalues are never accepted as proof.\n\nThis also covers cycles longer than triangles by introducing shared fill products. It does not impose rank one or complete cycle consistency. Cuts are added only as needed and obey the existing round/time budgets. Bound-tightening stages and saved certificates retain the clique configuration, so they rebuild the same sparse variables and inequalities. Older ledgers still reconstruct the original model with no cycle extension.\n\nlp = certify_feasibility_lp(problem; optimizer_factory=HiGHS.Optimizer,\n    psd_clique_limit=8, time_limit=60.0, max_rounds=100,\n    bound_tightening_rounds=3, bound_bus_limit=16)","title":"Bounded cycle strengthening"},{"category":"section","location":"global_feasibility/#Optional-solver-environment","page":"Global certificates","text":"The optional solvers are installed separately from the main package.\n\nThe optional environment uses HiGHS for LP search and SCIP for global search. SCIP supports spatial branch-and-cut for bounded MINLPs; this provides numerical global search, not an independently replayable nonlinear search-tree certificate in DroopOPF. See SCIP's problem classes and the Julia interface.\n\nresult = check_feasibility(problem;\n    initial_states = [source_state],\n    lp_options = (optimizer_factory=HiGHS.Optimizer,\n                  time_limit=30.0, bound_tightening_rounds=2),\n    global_options = (optimizer_factory=SCIP.Optimizer,\n                      target_bus=8, time_limit=60.0),\n)\n\nTo reuse verified bounds explicitly:\n\nlp = certify_feasibility_lp(problem; optimizer_factory=HiGHS.Optimizer,\n    time_limit=30.0, bound_tightening_rounds=2)\nif lp.status == :unresolved\n    global_analysis = solve_global_feasibility(problem;\n        optimizer_factory=SCIP.Optimizer, target_bus=8, max_relaxation=0.0,\n        voltage_bound_result=lp, time_limit=60.0)\nend\n\nBudgets cap LP refinement rounds, bound-tightening rounds, the number of buses selected for tightening, and solver time. Model compilation/construction and independent proof replay have additional overhead. Solver bounds, candidate points, controls, scope fingerprints and failed attempts remain in the ledgers.","title":"Optional solver environment"},{"category":"section","location":"global_feasibility/#Interpreting-incomplete-calculations","page":"Global certificates","text":"A time limit, zero lower bound or positive local relaxation value does not certify infeasibility. A feasible outer relaxation is not an AC witness. Sparse construction does not establish tractable global solution time. Retain the model contract, solver outcome, failed attempts and independently replayed proof or witness for each calculation.","title":"Interpreting incomplete calculations"},{"category":"section","location":"global_feasibility/#API","page":"Global certificates","text":"","title":"API"},{"category":"function","location":"global_feasibility/#DroopOPF.build_global_feasibility_model","page":"Global certificates","text":"Build a bounded voltage-restoration model for a frozen fixed-equipment problem.\n\nmode=:global uses rectangular AC voltages and exact SOS2 droop regions. mode=:soc uses sparse edge-product SOC envelopes and droop graph convex hulls. Only target_bus may exceed its original upper voltage bound, by delta in [0,max_relaxation]. Frozen feasibility tolerances are included in both models. The SOC model is an outer relaxation, never a feasible-point certificate. Angle windows outside (-pi/2,pi/2) are explicitly omitted (an outer relaxation). Pass an optional global/conic solver factory; no optimization occurs here.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.build_global_feasibility_model"},{"category":"function","location":"global_feasibility/#DroopOPF.seed_global_feasibility!","page":"Global certificates","text":"Map an AC point to every lifted variable and SOS2 weight, without changing the problem.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.seed_global_feasibility!"},{"category":"function","location":"global_feasibility/#DroopOPF.solve_global_feasibility","page":"Global certificates","text":"Run one explicitly budgeted global/SOC escalation and return its gate result and ledger.\n\nOnly independently replayed AC points can upgrade the gate here. Floating-point solver bounds are retained as solver_reported_infeasibility evidence, not promoted to independently verified certificates. This distinction also holds at time limits and when the solver reports OPTIMAL/INFEASIBLE.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.solve_global_feasibility"},{"category":"function","location":"global_feasibility/#DroopOPF.certify_feasibility_lp","page":"Global certificates","text":"Try a sparse outer LP and return only independently verified infeasibility.\n\nThe optional LP solver supplies candidate Farkas multipliers. The checker uses exact rational arithmetic, including residual stationarity errors over finite variable bounds. SOC/thermal tangent cuts are independently validated. Optional psd_clique_limit (0 disables; 3–32 enables) adds bounded-size cycle PSD support cuts, whose rational coefficients are reconstructed independently. Feasible LP points never qualify the original nonconvex problem. The work is bounded by time_limit and max_rounds; no global solver is required by the main package.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.certify_feasibility_lp"},{"category":"function","location":"global_feasibility/#DroopOPF.verified_feasibility_voltage_bounds","page":"Global certificates","text":"Replay every LP weak-duality bound proof and return outward-rounded voltage bounds.\n\nThis never trusts the ledger's displayed bounds. Bounds from the original strict problem must not be reused as bounds on a voltage-relaxed problem.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.verified_feasibility_voltage_bounds"},{"category":"section","location":"concepts/#What-problem-are-we-solving?","page":"What problem are we solving?","text":"","title":"What problem are we solving?"},{"category":"section","location":"concepts/#Start-with-an-operating-point","page":"What problem are we solving?","text":"An AC network has buses, connecting branches, generators and loads. An operating point gives each bus's voltage magnitude and angle and each generator's active and reactive output. Those numbers must satisfy AC power balance and equipment limits at the same time.\n\nQuantity Symbol Stored in\nBus voltage magnitude, in per unit v_b for bus b ACState.vm\nBus voltage angle, in radians delta_b ACState.va\nGenerator active output P_g for generator g ACState.pg\nGenerator reactive output Q_g ACState.qg\n\nThe subscript identifies a device; it is not necessarily an array position. Vectors follow the order in case.network.buses and case.generators.\n\nPower flow finds a consistent state under prescribed operating rules. Optimal power flow (OPF) also chooses permitted operating values to minimize an explicit objective. DroopOPF's default base objective measures departure from supplied generator reference outputs; it is not an invented fuel-cost curve. See the worked example for the exact objective and its units.","title":"Start with an operating point"},{"category":"section","location":"concepts/#A-controller-adds-an-equation","page":"What problem are we solving?","text":"A free reactive output can move anywhere within its limits to help the optimization. A droop-controlled output must also lie on its supplied voltage/output curve. Consequently, the optimizer cannot independently choose both measured voltage and reactive output for that generator. It solves their intersection with the network.\n\n(Image: Four reactive modes, showing the different equation added to the common AC network.)\n\nFigure 1. Each mode determines a different relation between reactive output and the network voltage. All modes still obey availability and capability constraints.","title":"A controller adds an equation"},{"category":"section","location":"concepts/#Four-reactive-modes","page":"What problem are we solving?","text":"Mode What it means What can respond\nFreeQ Reactive output is an optimization variable within limits. The optimizer selects Q_g.\nFixedQ Reactive output equals a supplied schedule. Voltage follows the network equations.\nAVR Automatic voltage regulation holds a supplied measured-voltage target while reactive capability permits. Q_g changes; at a reactive limit, voltage regulation releases.\nVoltVarDroop Reactive output follows a supplied deadband/saturated curve of measured voltage. Q_g and voltage move together along the curve.\n\nAn explicit ReactiveControlAssignment names the mode for a generator. Legacy GeneratorControlAttachments connect generators to entries in case.controls. A generator without a legacy droop attachment defaults to FreeQ; the presence of a generator alone does not create a droop or an AVR controller.\n\nThe regulated location can be a local bus, a remote bus or a supported branch terminal. It says where voltage is measured. It does not relocate the generator's power injection. Learn the data contract in the data model.","title":"Four reactive modes"},{"category":"section","location":"concepts/#Dispatch,-response-and-design-are-three-decisions","page":"What problem are we solving?","text":"Decision Example How it is represented\nPreventive dispatch Choose generator outputs before an outage. Base AC state.\nAutomatic response Surviving generators adjust active output, or a fixed reactive curve produces a new equilibrium. Declared inter-scenario policy and unchanged controller law.\nController/equipment design Select one slope, reference, tap or bank susceptance within specified bounds. Selected design parameters and their sharing/timing rules.\n\nA fixed droop curve can give different reactive outputs at different operating points. That is response, even though the curve itself has not been redesigned. A designed curve is shared across the declared training conditions; testing a new condition should freeze it according to the evaluation policy.","title":"Dispatch, response and design are three decisions"},{"category":"section","location":"concepts/#Security-and-time-add-different-kinds-of-coupling","page":"What problem are we solving?","text":"A contingency is a declared loss of a line, transformer or generator. Security-constrained OPF (SCOPF) finds a base operating point with acceptable states for its declared outages and response/control policy. An N-1 study considers individual device losses; it must state which devices and islanding conditions are actually covered.\n\nSeveral demand scenarios are alternative operating conditions. Several periods are ordered operating points with durations and, when supplied, dispatch-ramp constraints. Outage alternatives at a period are not a chronological simulation of the outage. See the scenario/time diagram.","title":"Security and time add different kinds of coupling"},{"category":"section","location":"concepts/#What-a-successful-result-establishes","page":"What problem are we solving?","text":"Native solver convergence and independent physical/policy replay are both needed. They support the particular modeled equilibrium and declared conditions. Local nonlinear optimization does not establish a global optimum. Steady-state feasibility does not establish transient stability, frequency response, a device switching trajectory or discrete tap/bank implementability.\n\nNext: build and check a two-bus OPF.","title":"What a successful result establishes"},{"category":"section","location":"equipment_optimization/#Equipment-and-joint-droop-optimization","page":"Equipment optimization","text":"Selected continuous taps, simple banks and curve parameters can be optimized while unselected equipment stays at supplied settings. Start with branch/shunt physics and the data contract. Bounds must describe the equipment you intend to permit; imported settings do not establish manufacturer capability or legal positions.\n\n(Image: From-side tap, series impedance and branch charging in the electrical model.)\n\nFigure 1. Tap design changes the positive from-side ratio. Bank design changes bus admittance, which is separate from branch charging.","title":"Equipment and joint droop optimization"},{"category":"section","location":"equipment_optimization/#Transformer-ratios","page":"Equipment optimization","text":"For a case with transformer ID 11 and a validated state:\n\ncontrols = [TapControl(11; lower=0.95, upper=1.05, nominal=1.0)]\nresult = optimize_taps(case, controls; initial_state=state)\naudit = validate_tap_design(case, result)\nphysical_case = with_tap_settings(case, result.taps)\n\nThe bounds and nominal are dimensionless ratios. initial is a numerical starting value; nominal is a reporting reference, not an objective penalty. Set equal lower/upper bounds to fix a selected setting. Phase shifts remain supplied. Both branch-terminal ratings and AC balance remain active.","title":"Transformer ratios"},{"category":"section","location":"equipment_optimization/#Simple-bank-susceptance","page":"Equipment optimization","text":"A selected bank must have supported single-step metadata with nonzero step susceptance. For example, step susceptance B_step=002 pu and legal counts 0–3 give a continuous design interval 0–0.06 pu. Here B_step is admittance per section, while B is aggregate susceptance. With conductance per section G_step, aggregate conductance remains G=G_stepBB_step. Positive B is capacitive; negative B is inductive.\n\n# Requires supported simple bank ID 201 in case.network.banks.\nresult = optimize_shunts(case, [ShuntControl(201)]; initial_state=state)\naudit = validate_shunt_design(case, result)\nphysical_case = with_shunt_settings(case, result.susceptances)\n\nA fractional section count is a continuous relaxation. It needs a separate legal state recovery and validation before being described as discrete switching. Fixed shunts without bank metadata cannot be selected through this interface.","title":"Simple bank susceptance"},{"category":"section","location":"equipment_optimization/#Joint-settings-and-curves","page":"Equipment optimization","text":"result = optimize_joint_design(case;\n    tap_controls=[TapControl(11; lower=0.95, upper=1.05)],\n    shunt_controls=[ShuntControl(201)],\n    droop_controls=[DroopControl(2; slope_bounds=(0.04,0.10))],\n    initial_state=state)\naudit = validate_joint_design(case, result)\nphysical_case = with_joint_settings(case, result)\n\nThis fragment assumes those device IDs and control index 2 exist. Slope is in pu voltage/pu reactive power. Missing design bounds fix the corresponding curve parameter. Reactive output inside the deadband and supplied capability remain fixed. Explicit reactive assignments must match the selected design family.\n\nCompare fixed and free configurations under the same objective, operating limits and initial/evaluation policy. Similar objectives with different settings can indicate weak parameter identification; repeated starts do not prove a unique or global optimum.","title":"Joint settings and curves"},{"category":"section","location":"equipment_optimization/#Outage-permission-is-a-separate-decision","page":"Equipment optimization","text":"A base Case design does not itself authorize corrective equipment movement. A Study declares selections, absolute bounds, preventive/corrective timing and movement maps through SCOPFControlPolicy. Read SCOPF equipment timing before using outage recourse. Continuous endpoints do not establish device timing or the feasibility of the path between endpoints.","title":"Outage permission is a separate decision"},{"category":"section","location":"solvers/#Solver-formulations","page":"Solver formulations","text":"Read the physical curve before choosing its numerical encoding. Both encodings below aim at the same supplied control law. They differ in how corners are represented, not in the meaning of equipment capability.","title":"Solver formulations"},{"category":"section","location":"solvers/#Symbols-used-here","page":"Solver formulations","text":"V is measured voltage, Vref reference voltage, Qref deadband reactive output, Qraw unsaturated request and slope positive voltage-per-reactive-power slope. Voltage and power quantities use their native pu units. epsilon0 denotes a numerical smoothing width; z is a scalar input in the same units as that width. uw below are auxiliary hinge value/slack, also in those units.","title":"Symbols used here"},{"category":"section","location":"solvers/#Smooth-nonlinear-programming","page":"Solver formulations","text":"solve_opf encodes the positive-part terms in the volt-var graph with stable softplus functions based on LogExpFunctions.log1pexp. The mathematical positive-part approximation is\n\ns_epsilon(z)=epsilonlog(1+exp(zepsilon))\n longrightarrow max(z0)quadtextas epsilonto0^+\n\nHere s_epsilon is the smooth hinge, log the natural logarithm and exp the exponential. The implementation uses a stable evaluation instead of forming a potentially overflowing exponential directly. Ipopt is the default:\n\nipopt_result = solve_opf(case; smooth_epsilon = 1.0e-3)\n\nMadNLP receives the same JuMP model and can be selected through the optimizer factory:\n\nusing MadNLP\nmadnlp_result = solve_opf(\n    case;\n    optimizer_factory = MadNLP.Optimizer,\n    smooth_epsilon = 1.0e-3,\n)\n\nThe voltage smoothing width is in per-unit voltage. By default, reactive smoothing is a relative fraction of each control's available reactive range; smooth_reactive_epsilon can override it with an absolute per-unit width.","title":"Smooth nonlinear programming"},{"category":"section","location":"solvers/#Exact-complementarity-formulation","page":"Solver formulations","text":"solve_opf_complementarity uses MathOptComplements and CCOpt. Each voltage hinge is represented as a nonnegative complementarity pair: uge0, w=u-zge0 and uw=0 imply u=max(z0). This is often written 0le uperp wge0; the perpendicular symbol means zero product here. Reactive clipping is represented by the Karush–Kuhn–Tucker (KKT) conditions for projection of the raw droop value onto the control's reactive interval.\n\nWhen both deadband widths are fixed at exactly zero, the raw response is Qraw = Qref + (Vref - V) / slope. The model omits the two voltage-hinge pairs and their four auxiliary variables; the two Q-projection pairs remain. This applies to fixed curves and to designs with free slope/reference and fixed zero widths. Positive or variable widths retain the general hinge formulation. The smooth backend uses the same affine raw response and retains its reactive saturation smoothing. This is algebraically equivalent to subtracting the two voltage softplus terms, including derivatives.\n\nccopt_encoding_audit marks these controllers with voltage_hinges_eliminated=true; their hinge errors are zero by construction. Raw-response, projection and independent exact-droop errors remain reported.\n\nccopt_result = solve_opf_complementarity(case)\n\nThe exact formulation has no smoothing parameter. CCOpt may report ALMOST_LOCALLY_SOLVED after its homotopy; use the independent equilibrium report and complementarity_residual_max to assess the result.","title":"Exact complementarity formulation"},{"category":"section","location":"solvers/#Comparing-solutions","page":"Solver formulations","text":"Ipopt and MadNLP should usually agree closely because they solve the same smoothed NLP. CCOpt solves the exact PWL graph, so compare physical residuals, objectives, operating regimes, and state differences rather than expecting bitwise equality. The reproducible comparison is:\n\njulia --project=. examples/m1_solver_comparison.jl\n\nSee Solver compatibility for recommended starting tolerances.","title":"Comparing solutions"},{"category":"section","location":"solvers/#Solver-compatibility","page":"Solver formulations","text":"Capability Ipopt MadNLP CCOpt\nSingle-case OPF smooth, tested smooth, tested exact, tested\nFixed-curve SCOPF smooth, tested smooth, tested with a validated warm start exact, tested\nSingle-controller parameter design smooth, tested smooth, tested with a validated warm start use the multi-controller exact API instead\nReconstructed fixed curve smooth re-solve available smooth re-solve available exact re-solve, tested\n\nThe fixed-curve SCOPF/droop design fixture is nonconvex and MadNLP can select an infeasible local point from its default start at the tight 1e-5 smoothing width. The compatibility test therefore warm-starts MadNLP from the independently validated Ipopt fixed-curve SCOPF states. Both smooth solvers then validate and reach the same droop design objective, but their optimized parameter values need not match because the design is weakly identifiable.\n\nThe single-controller optimize_droop_parameters(study, control_id; ...) overload rejects CCOpt.Optimizer: its parameterized model is a smooth NLP. The multi-controller overload accepts optimize_droop_parameters(study, controls; encoding=:complementarity, ...) for an exact CCOpt design. Omit optimizer_factory in exact mode. The same encoding can be selected on optimize_droop_security; its final scenario assessments retain the master's encoding. Nonzero deadbands and saturation remain explicit complementarity constraints, and exact design still requires independent physical and policy validation. Shared exact design currently uses the case attachments; explicit reactive_assignments are rejected on this parameterized exact path. After reconstruction with with_droop_settings, use validate_droop_design for exact PWL state replay, which is independent of all three solvers, or use solve_scopf(...; encoding=:complementarity) for a CCOpt exact fixed-curve re-solve. Solver termination alone is never the acceptance test; the resulting state must pass equilibrium_report.","title":"Solver compatibility"},{"category":"section","location":"study_workflows/#From-one-equilibrium-to-secure-and-multi-period-studies","page":"Outages, demands and periods","text":"Goal: choose what stays shared before choosing an optimization API. The AC state is x=(vdeltaPQ): bus voltage magnitudes/angles and generator active/reactive outputs. Let theta denote selected curve/equipment design parameters; these are distinct from the AC state. Subscript c identifies an outage scenario, with c=0 the base. Subscript k identifies a chronological period. All powers are in pu unless stated otherwise.","title":"From one equilibrium to secure and multi-period studies"},{"category":"section","location":"study_workflows/#One-case,-several-outage-equilibria","page":"Outages, demands and periods","text":"(Image: One shared control policy feeding a base AC state and two alternative outage states.)\n\nFigure 1. States differ by outage topology/availability, while parameter sharing and the base-to-outage response policy constrain what can change.\n\nA fixed-curve Study has one base dispatch and a declared contingency list. Each state x_c satisfies its own network equations, device bounds and reactive law. Generator or branch unavailability removes that device's injection or flow. A generator's curve can stay fixed while its Q output changes with voltage.\n\nQuestion Choose explicitly\nWhich devices can fail? The contingency catalogue, including excluded/unsupported islanding entries.\nWhich active units can respond? Participation weights, effective capability and declared movement bounds.\nCan equipment change after the outage? Preventive/corrective timing, selected devices, absolute bounds and movement budgets.\nCan the curve change? A fixed curve stays fixed; shared design selects one parameter vector for the declared training states.\nWhat counts as completion? Required catalogue coverage, native convergence and independent state/policy replay.\n\nThe complete security example runs this coupling on a three-bus network. The SCOPF guide specifies exact API behavior. A ranking merely changes execution order; an unassessed low-ranked outage is not an accepted AC equilibrium.","title":"One case, several outage equilibria"},{"category":"section","location":"study_workflows/#Understand-limit-aware-active-response","page":"Outages, demands and periods","text":"For generator g in scenario c, P_g0 is base output, alpha_gcge0 its normalized survivor weight, lambda_c the common request signal and L_gcU_gc effective active bounds, all power quantities in pu. The preventive response law is\n\nP_gc=operatornameclip(P_g0+alpha_gclambda_cL_gcU_gc)\n\nClipping is operatornameclip(zLU)=min(Umax(Lz)). Here z is a real input and LU its lower/upper bounds. Effective bounds intersect equipment capability and declared base-to-outage movement restrictions. Unavailable units have zero output. Zero-weight units hold their base output. Default weights use nonzero effective active-capability ranges among eligible survivors; actual supplied response weights take precedence.\n\n(Image: Active-response change versus common signal: one participant saturates while another continues along its permitted response.)\n\nFigure 2. Illustrative upward response. Delta P is actual output minus base output; lambda is the request signal. The optimizer can increase the signal until remaining participants deliver the required balance, subject to their bounds.\n\nOnce a unit clips, lambda_c need not equal the sum of delivered changes. Compute actual delivery from the output differences. If headroom is inadequate, the model cannot create missing supply. The weights encode a steady-state sharing assumption, not governor speed or calibrated frequency dynamics.\n\nA corrective active-redispatch study instead bounds each generator's allowed output change directly. Its symmetric limit is a movement allowance in pu, not a ramp rate. Do not confuse this active policy with corrective tap/shunt timing.","title":"Understand limit-aware active response"},{"category":"section","location":"study_workflows/#Preventive-versus-corrective-equipment","page":"Outages, demands and periods","text":"A preventive selected tap has one setting shared across base/outage states where it is available. A corrective selected tap can have a different post-outage setting inside declared absolute and movement bounds. An unselected tap stays fixed. The same distinction applies to selected shunt controls.\n\nAPI defaults for selected equipment are documented in SCOPF timing. Declare timing explicitly in a study protocol. A library's nothing movement map and an empty map have different meanings: no extra movement cap versus no listed corrective devices. Continuous settings and settled-state permission do not imply physical delays, tap steps, discrete switching or a valid transition trajectory.","title":"Preventive versus corrective equipment"},{"category":"section","location":"study_workflows/#Alternative-demands-versus-chronological-periods","page":"Outages, demands and periods","text":"Independent demand cases have no implied order or ramp relation. A multi-period study has an ordered set mathcal T of period starts. Each period k has a positive duration Delta t_k in hours and its own state. Supplied ramp rates r_g^upr_g^down are in pu/hour and constrain preventive dispatch as\n\n-r_g^downDelta t_kle P_gk+1-P_gk\nle r_g^upDelta t_k\n\nP_gk is base active output in period k. The final duration weights energy only. Initial/terminal or cyclic ramp boundaries must not be inferred.\n\n(Image: Three chronological periods with preventive-dispatch ramp arrows and alternative outage rows; common droop parameters span the grid.)\n\nFigure 3. Horizontal arrows impose planned base dispatch ramps. Vertical layouts show alternative outages, not simulation time. Shared curves span the specified grid.\n\nThe base-operation multi-period objective is active loss energy:\n\nE_loss=S_basesum_kinmathcal TDelta t_k\n left(sum_ginmathcal GP_gk-sum_linmathcal LP^L_lkright)\n\nHere S_base is the power base in MVA, mathcal G the generator set, mathcal L the load set and P^L_lk the active consumption of load l in period k. Per-unit active power times this base is MW; multiplying by hours gives MWh. Loss includes shunt conductance. It is not fuel cost.\n\noptimize_multi_period is the fixed-equipment base-operation slice. optimize_multi_period_security adds declared outage recourse and its study policies. A base-only profile does not establish security at every period or dynamic security between periods.","title":"Alternative demands versus chronological periods"},{"category":"section","location":"study_workflows/#A-practical-study-sequence","page":"Outages, demands and periods","text":"Validate a single fixed-control operating point with the worked tutorial.\nWrite the control freedoms, capability, response weights and equipment timing.\nCompare supplied curves with a bounded shared design under the same operating limits.\nFreeze the design for the declared evaluation cases; keep any demand-specific preventive dispatch freedom explicit.\nRun the complete required catalogue and replay every accepted result.\nReport unresolved solves, exclusions and resource cost alongside accepted coverage.\n\nThese steps explain why adding scenarios changes the optimization problem without turning it into a dynamic simulation or a global-optimality certificate.","title":"A practical study sequence"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#API-reference","page":"API","text":"","title":"API reference"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.AVR","page":"API","text":"Automatic voltage regulation with generator Q limits supplied by the case.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.AVR"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.Branch","page":"API","text":"Two-terminal line or transformer with a from-side complex tap tap_ratio * cis(phase_shift). kind is explicit :line or :transformer metadata; it does not alter the electrical equations. The ratio is positive and dimensionless; phase shift is in radians. Ratio and phase are fixed equipment inputs in transformer support.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.Branch"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.ComplementarityOPFResult","page":"API","text":"Result returned by solve_opf_complementarity.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.ComplementarityOPFResult"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.Contingency","page":"API","text":"A named outage overlay. Device IDs refer to the original case; :base is reserved.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.Contingency"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.ContingencyAssessment","page":"API","text":"One independently validated fixed-base evaluation from a contingency catalog.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.ContingencyAssessment"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.ContingencyCatalog","page":"API","text":"A deterministic, provenance-preserving N-1 candidate set for one case.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.ContingencyCatalog"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.ContingencyCatalogEntry","page":"API","text":"One candidate N-1 outage together with its structural disposition.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.ContingencyCatalogEntry"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.ContingencyFilterDecision","page":"API","text":"One conservative pre-solve routing decision.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.ContingencyFilterDecision"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.ContingencyPipelineAssessment","page":"API","text":"One routed contingency and any evaluation retained for it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.ContingencyPipelineAssessment"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.ContingencyPipelineRank","page":"API","text":"Unified operational rank for screened, exact, and island-aware outcomes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.ContingencyPipelineRank"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.ContingencyPipelineResult","page":"API","text":"Complete, coverage-checked result of the contingency assessment pipeline.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.ContingencyPipelineResult"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.ContingencyRank","page":"API","text":"One exact-evaluation severity ranking; it never asserts that omitted cases are safe.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.ContingencyRank"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.ContingencyScreeningScore","page":"API","text":"A cheap pre-solve priority score; it is not a safety classification.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.ContingencyScreeningScore"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.DroopBreakpointDiagnostic","page":"API","text":"Distance from one active controller operating point to its nearest PWL breakpoint.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.DroopBreakpointDiagnostic"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.DroopControl","page":"API","text":"Independent bounds for the existing droop design droop family; omitted fields stay fixed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.DroopControl"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.DroopDesignMultiStartResult","page":"API","text":"droop design parameter multi-start comparison retaining every design attempt.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.DroopDesignMultiStartResult"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.DroopDesignMultiStartRun","page":"API","text":"Optimized droop design design and independent validation from one parameter start.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.DroopDesignMultiStartRun"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.DroopDesignResult","page":"API","text":"droop design optimized settings, reference objective, and scenario-indexed SCOPF result.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.DroopDesignResult"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.DroopParameterSpread","page":"API","text":"Component-wise spread across independently valid optimized settings.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.DroopParameterSpread"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.DroopQualification","page":"API","text":"Candidate droops and all required calibration/demand feasibility decisions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.DroopQualification"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.DroopSecurityIteration","page":"API","text":"One design round, including all executed independent assessments and errors.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.DroopSecurityIteration"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.DroopSecurityResult","page":"API","text":"Contingency-addition result; eligible acceptance and full N-1 coverage differ.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.DroopSecurityResult"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.DroopSettings","page":"API","text":"Physical settings for the optimizable standard volt-var droop family.\n\ndeadband_low and deadband_high are nonnegative widths below and above v_ref; they may differ or both be fixed at zero for a no-deadband curve.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.DroopSettings"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.DroopSlopeSweep","page":"API","text":"A reference-anchored sweep of one control's positive droop slope.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.DroopSlopeSweep"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.DroopSlopeSweepPoint","page":"API","text":"Metrics and solver status for one fixed droop slope in an droop design parameter sweep.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.DroopSlopeSweepPoint"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.EquilibriumValidationReport","page":"API","text":"Independent checks for a solved AC equilibrium.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.EquilibriumValidationReport"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.FeasibilityProblem","page":"API","text":"A frozen, base-case feasibility contract. Omitted taps/banks remain fixed.\n\nSelected banks are continuous susceptances within their specified envelope. angle_limits maps branch IDs to (minimum, maximum) bus-angle differences in radians. All physical checks use exact PWL droops, regardless of search encoding. The constructor copies the inputs; subsequent mutation of the snapshot is rejected. This is not a coupled SCOPF contract.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.FeasibilityProblem"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.FeasibilityResult","page":"API","text":"Tri-state decision and its witness or replayable certificate, with a search ledger.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.FeasibilityResult"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.FeasibilityWitness","page":"API","text":"An AC state and the actual equipment settings at that state; no solver claim.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.FeasibilityWitness"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.FixedQ","page":"API","text":"Generator Q is fixed to an explicit schedule.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.FixedQ"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.FixedShunt","page":"API","text":"Fixed bus admittance in per unit: G >= 0 consumes P; B > 0 injects Q. Terminal consumption is (conductance - im*susceptance)*abs2(V). Availability disables the equipment without losing its supplied parameters. This is not a switched-bank model; no legal positions or controls are inferred.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.FixedShunt"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.FreeQ","page":"API","text":"Generator Q is bounded only by its declared capability.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.FreeQ"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.IslandSelection","page":"API","text":"Selection and accounting for the component retained after an islanding outage.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.IslandSelection"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.IslandedContingencyAssessment","page":"API","text":"Reduced-network OPF and independent validation for one islanding outage.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.IslandedContingencyAssessment"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.MultiPeriodResult","page":"API","text":"Native multi-period result: per-period states/loss MW, shared droop settings, and total duration-weighted loss MWh. Acceptance requires validate_multi_period.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.MultiPeriodResult"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.MultiPeriodSecurityResult","page":"API","text":"Multi-period AC security result. Contingency states are settled responses; there is no transient trajectory or inter-period ramp on outage states.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.MultiPeriodSecurityResult"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.MultiPeriodStudy","page":"API","text":"Ordered, deterministic AC periods with fixed equipment and common droop curves.\n\nduration_hours[t] weights period t's energy and is the elapsed time from period t to t+1. ramp_limits[id] = (up, down) is in pu/hour on the case MVA base. Omitted generators have no inter-period ramp restriction. There is no initial/terminal boundary, storage, commitment, uncertainty or outage recourse. Only loads may differ between period cases; their IDs and buses must agree.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.MultiPeriodStudy"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.PSSEImport","page":"API","text":"Strict AC import plus unmodified supplied operating-point data and source-ID maps.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.PSSEImport"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.PSSERawData","page":"API","text":"Parsed full-case RAW revision 33, 34 or 36; source text and unknown sections are retained. This is bus/branch RAW, not binary SAV, DYR, SEQ or RAWX. Parsing does not certify that every equipment model can be represented by the DroopOPF AC domain.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.PSSERawData"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.PSSERecord","page":"API","text":"One PSS/E RAW record, with typed known fields, original token rows and source lines. Opaque sections retain token rows without assigning electrical semantics.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.PSSERecord"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.PiecewiseLinearCurve","page":"API","text":"A strictly increasing, piecewise-linear scalar curve.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.PiecewiseLinearCurve"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.ReactiveControlAssignment","page":"API","text":"Attach one explicit reactive-control mode to a generator.\n\nFreeQ and FixedQ act at the generator and therefore take no regulated location. AVR and VoltVarDroop require a declared voltage location.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.ReactiveControlAssignment"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.ReactiveControlMode","page":"API","text":"Declared reactive-control semantics for one generator.\n\nThese modes are deliberately independent of their numerical encoding. AVR may later be represented by a smooth transition or exact complementarity without changing the physical assignment.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.ReactiveControlMode"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.SCOPFBenchmarkReport","page":"API","text":"Reproducible environment, structural size, and samples for one SCOPF study.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.SCOPFBenchmarkReport"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.SCOPFBenchmarkSample","page":"API","text":"One measured SCOPF solve, including actual JuMP model size and allocations.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.SCOPFBenchmarkSample"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.SCOPFControlPolicy","page":"API","text":"SCOPFControlPolicy(; taps=:corrective, shunts=:corrective, reactive=:automatic,\n    tap_movement_limits=nothing, shunt_movement_limits=nothing)\n\nDeclare cross-scenario timing. Taps and shunts default to corrective settings selected independently within their control bounds. Preventive equipment has one shared setting. Reactive assignments and voltage targets remain shared, while Q and voltage respond to each scenario's equations and availability.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.SCOPFControlPolicy"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.SCOPFDiagnostics","page":"API","text":"Structured SCOPF diagnostics kept separate from the stable validation report.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.SCOPFDiagnostics"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.SCOPFDroopDesignResult","page":"API","text":"Shared multi-controller droop design and its ordinary SCOPF scenario states.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.SCOPFDroopDesignResult"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.SCOPFFinding","page":"API","text":"A machine-readable robustness checks finding tied to a scenario and optional network object.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.SCOPFFinding"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.SCOPFMultiStartResult","page":"API","text":"Comparison of named SCOPF starts without discarding divergent or invalid runs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.SCOPFMultiStartResult"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.SCOPFMultiStartRun","page":"API","text":"One independently solved and validated SCOPF start.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.SCOPFMultiStartRun"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.SCOPFReport","page":"API","text":"Independent per-scenario physical and inter-scenario response checks.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.SCOPFReport"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.SCOPFResult","page":"API","text":"Scenario-indexed SCOPF output; :base is always present. States may be missing on failure. balancing_power stores the common active-response request signal; after saturation it differs from total delivered adjustment and can be nonunique.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.SCOPFResult"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.SCOPFStageAssessment","page":"API","text":"Paired immediate and settled steady-state SCOPF assessments sharing one fixed base.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.SCOPFStageAssessment"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.ShuntBank","page":"API","text":"Shunt bank with explicitly supplied legal step-count combinations. Step admittances use the fixed-shunt G/B convention. State and nominal_state are integer count tuples, not optimization variables. Metadata is copied to immutable tuples; unavailable banks retain their legal state but contribute zero admittance.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.ShuntBank"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.ShuntControl","page":"API","text":"Select a simple bank for continuous susceptance optimization.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.ShuntControl"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.Study","page":"API","text":"Study(case; contingencies=[], mode=:preventive, participation=nothing, redispatch_limits=Dict(),\n      tap_controls=[], shunt_controls=[], control_policy=SCOPFControlPolicy())\n\nFixed-curve SCOPF study. Preventive automatic response follows weighted requests clipped to each participant's effective active bounds and optional movement limits. A common response signal lets unsaturated generators take over unmet response. Default weights use nonzero effective active capability ranges; explicit weights override them. Zero-weight generators hold their base output. The signal is power in p.u., not frequency, and differs from total delivered response after saturation. Corrective response permits abs(P[c,g] - P[base,g]) <= redispatch_limits[g]. Missing limits mean zero redispatch. Limits also optionally cap preventive response. All powers use the case per-unit base. Generator capability limits apply in every scenario. Outaged generators have zero P/Q and no active droop equation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.Study"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.TapControl","page":"API","text":"Explicit continuous tap policy. Omitted branches remain fixed at supplied ratios.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.TapControl"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.VoltVarDroop","page":"API","text":"A static volt-var droop with exact PWL semantics and constant Q capability.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.VoltVarDroop"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF._branch_admittances-Tuple{Branch}","page":"API","text":"Two-terminal admittance primitive for a fixed, from-side complex tap.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF._branch_admittances"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF._build_complementarity_opf_model-Tuple{Case}","page":"API","text":"Build an exact PWL-droop AC OPF model for CCOpt.\n\nEach voltage hinge is represented as z = 0  z - a = 0. Reactive clipping is the KKT system of projection of the raw droop value onto the reactive capability interval. Positive-width deadbands use four pairs per attachment. Constant zero-width deadbands use the affine raw response directly and only the two reactive projection pairs, avoiding redundant voltage hinges.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF._build_complementarity_opf_model"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF._matpower_array-Tuple{AbstractString, AbstractString}","page":"API","text":"Minimal MATPOWER v2 reader for balanced AC network data.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF._matpower_array"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.assemble_scopf_response_stages-Tuple{Study, Study, SCOPFResult, SCOPFResult}","page":"API","text":"assemble_scopf_response_stages(immediate_study, settled_study, immediate, settled; ...)\n\nAssemble and independently validate a paired response-stage assessment from already solved results. This is the resumable/process-isolated counterpart to assess_scopf_response_stages.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.assemble_scopf_response_stages"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.assess_contingency_pipeline-Tuple{Case, ACState}","page":"API","text":"assess_contingency_pipeline(case, base_state; ...)\n\nRun a coverage-checked contingency assessment assessment pipeline. Every catalog entry receives exactly one route. audit_screened_ids names screened entries that must also receive exact evaluation. Set screening_enabled=false for an exact connected- outage baseline. Evaluation errors are retained unless continue_on_error=false. workers=1 preserves serial execution. Larger values use bounded threaded batches with independent models, serial native solves, and deterministic coordinator callbacks/checkpoints. Start Julia with enough threads. Phase timings sum worker durations and are not parallel wall time. User model hooks, if supplied, must be thread-safe.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.assess_contingency_pipeline"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.assess_scopf_response_stages-Tuple{Study, Study, SCOPFResult}","page":"API","text":"assess_scopf_response_stages(immediate_study, settled_study, base_result; ...)\n\nHold the validated base state and equipment settings fixed, then solve the same contingencies twice: first with preventive equipment frozen and then with the declared settled policy. These are paired equilibria, not a transient simulation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.assess_scopf_response_stages"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.attach_controls-Tuple{Case, AbstractVector{<:VoltVarDroop}, AbstractVector{<:GeneratorControlAttachment}}","page":"API","text":"Return a copy of case with the supplied base OPF generator controls attached.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.attach_controls"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.bank_admittance-Union{Tuple{ShuntBank{T}}, Tuple{T}} where T","page":"API","text":"Aggregate admittance at the supplied bank state; zero when unavailable.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.bank_admittance"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.bank_powers-Tuple{ACNetwork, ACState}","page":"API","text":"Bank terminal consumption in network.banks order, independently summed per step.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.bank_powers"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.benchmark_scopf-Tuple{Study}","page":"API","text":"benchmark_scopf(study; samples=3, warmup=true, ...)\n\nMeasure complete model construction, optimization, and result extraction. The optional warm-up solve is excluded. Allocation bytes are Julia allocations, not process peak RSS. process_peak_rss_bytes separately records the operating system's lifetime process high-water mark, including compilation, warm-up, solver native memory, and validation. It is not a per-solve increment. Run different studies in fresh processes for comparable peaks. Model size is counted from the actual JuMP model passed to the optimizer. Solver keywords are forwarded to solve_scopf.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.benchmark_scopf"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.best_droop_slope-Tuple{DroopSlopeSweep}","page":"API","text":"Return the valid sweep point with the smallest fixed-curve SCOPF dispatch objective.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.best_droop_slope"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.build_global_feasibility_model-Tuple{FeasibilityProblem}","page":"API","text":"Build a bounded voltage-restoration model for a frozen fixed-equipment problem.\n\nmode=:global uses rectangular AC voltages and exact SOS2 droop regions. mode=:soc uses sparse edge-product SOC envelopes and droop graph convex hulls. Only target_bus may exceed its original upper voltage bound, by delta in [0,max_relaxation]. Frozen feasibility tolerances are included in both models. The SOC model is an outer relaxation, never a feasible-point certificate. Angle windows outside (-pi/2,pi/2) are explicitly omitted (an outer relaxation). Pass an optional global/conic solver factory; no optimization occurs here.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.build_global_feasibility_model"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.ccopt_diagnostics-Tuple{JuMP.Model}","page":"API","text":"Read final CCOpt/MadNLP relaxation diagnostics from a solved JuMP model.\n\nThe iteration count is the cumulative inner MadNLP count exposed by CCOpt. The package does not currently expose a separate outer-homotopy iteration count. These are final relaxed-NLP metrics, not an original-MPCC stationarity proof. When CCOpt delegates a model without complementarity constraints to MadNLP, the pair count is zero and relaxation and CCOpt-only timing fields are nothing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.ccopt_diagnostics"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.ccopt_encoding_audit-Tuple{JuMP.Model}","page":"API","text":"Compare CCOpt's solved auxiliary MPCC variables with direct exact-droop evaluation.\n\nThis is a read-only diagnostic for distinguishing a relaxed-complementarity iterate from an extraction/model-bridge discrepancy. It does not change the model, stopping rules, or physical acceptance criteria.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.ccopt_encoding_audit"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.certify_feasibility_lp-Tuple{FeasibilityProblem}","page":"API","text":"Try a sparse outer LP and return only independently verified infeasibility.\n\nThe optional LP solver supplies candidate Farkas multipliers. The checker uses exact rational arithmetic, including residual stationarity errors over finite variable bounds. SOC/thermal tangent cuts are independently validated. Optional psd_clique_limit (0 disables; 3–32 enables) adds bounded-size cycle PSD support cuts, whose rational coefficients are reconstructed independently. Feasible LP points never qualify the original nonconvex problem. The work is bounded by time_limit and max_rounds; no global solver is required by the main package.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.certify_feasibility_lp"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.check_feasibility-Tuple{FeasibilityProblem}","page":"API","text":"Replay seeds, check global necessary conditions, then optionally search locally.\n\nA local failure, positive local restoration objective, or absence of a witness returns :unresolved. :certified_infeasible requires an independently replayed certificate. Optional lp_options and global_options select bounded escalation backends; neither runs by default. Solver-reported global bounds alone do not upgrade the gate. Inconclusive certificates or unsupported backends are unresolved.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.check_feasibility"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.check_psse_file-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"API","text":"Check a RAW saved power-flow solution and optionally save a JSON report.\n\nThis check-only path never exposes a case with invented thermal ratings. It uses an internal finite adapter placeholder for zero/omitted ratings solely to replay AC physics, excludes those placeholders from every thermal check, and reports incomplete thermal coverage. Ordinary load_psse_case still requires an explicit unrated_limit before it can return such a case. Unsupported data return status=\"unsupported\"; malformed input returns status=\"invalid_file\".\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.check_psse_file"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.contingency_catalog-Tuple{Case}","page":"API","text":"contingency_catalog(case; include_branches=true, include_generators=true)\n\nEnumerate every available single branch and generator outage without silently discarding candidates. Branch outages that island the network are retained as :excluded with reason :islanding_unsupported, because the current SCOPF formulation has one reference angle and does not support island balancing. Generator outages and connected line/transformer outages are :eligible.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.contingency_catalog"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.contingency_screening_scores-Tuple{Case, ACState}","page":"API","text":"contingency_screening_scores(case, state; catalog=contingency_catalog(case))\n\nPrioritize eligible outages using base-state branch loading within the branch family and active-power share within the generator family. Scores are empirical within-family percentiles so unlike raw quantities can share one queue. This is a screening feature only: low score never means safe.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.contingency_screening_scores"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.default_participation-Tuple{Case}","page":"API","text":"default_participation(case; reactive_assignments=nothing)\n\nCapability-range weights for available generators with nonzero effective active range. These are a declared fallback, not measured governor/AGC response data. Supply explicit nonnegative weights when actual response data are available.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.default_participation"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.equilibrium_report-Tuple{Case, ACOPFResult}","page":"API","text":"Combine solver metadata with an independent equilibrium validation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.equilibrium_report"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.evaluate_contingencies-Tuple{Study, ACState}","page":"API","text":"Evaluate all contingencies while holding the supplied base equilibrium fixed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.evaluate_contingencies"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.evaluate_contingency_catalog-Tuple{Case, ACState}","page":"API","text":"evaluate_contingency_catalog(case, base_state; catalog, participation, kwargs...)\n\nEvaluate every structurally eligible catalog entry as an independent fixed-base problem. The base state must independently validate. Excluded entries remain in the catalog and are never silently treated as safe.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.evaluate_contingency_catalog"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.evaluate_held_out_contingencies-Tuple{Study, DroopDesignResult, AbstractVector{<:Contingency}}","page":"API","text":"Evaluate an optimized base dispatch against contingencies excluded from training.\n\nReturns a named tuple containing the held-out study, result, and independent report. Held-out IDs must differ from the training scenario IDs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.evaluate_held_out_contingencies"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.evaluate_islanding_contingency-Tuple{Case, Contingency, ACState}","page":"API","text":"Solve and independently validate the retained component of an islanding outage.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.evaluate_islanding_contingency"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.exact_droop_audit-Tuple{Case, JointDesignResult}","page":"API","text":"Independently audit each controlled generator against its exact physical droop curve.\n\nFor a complementarity result this is an extraction check, not a second solve: it compares returned qg values with direct evaluation of the exact PWL curve after applying any optimized tap, shunt, and droop settings.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.exact_droop_audit"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.feasibility_fingerprint-Tuple{FeasibilityProblem}","page":"API","text":"Return the scope SHA-256, rejecting mutation of any frozen physical data.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.feasibility_fingerprint"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.filter_contingencies-Tuple{Case, ACState}","page":"API","text":"filter_contingencies(case, base_state; loading_threshold=0.8, voltage_guard=0.02)\n\nRoute N-1 candidates before exact AC evaluation. Generator outages always go to exact evaluation. Islanding branches go to the island-aware path. Connected line and transformer outages use a lossless DC LODF prediction; numerical uncertainty and a tight base-voltage margin fall back to exact evaluation. reactive_guard=0.1 escalates when aggregate bidirectional Q headroom is at most 10% of available Q range. ac_risk_guard=true also checks apparent-flow redistribution and an impedance-based incremental voltage-drop indicator. These are escalation heuristics, not AC power-flow solutions or bounds. Set reactive_guard=0, ac_risk_guard=false to replay the legacy policy. Opt-in droop_response_guard=true adds individual Q-reserve and bounded AC/droop-response risk checks for fixed-equipment legacy droops. Unsupported or uncertain predictions escalate to exact AC; the default remains unchanged. participation must match the subsequent fixed-base exact evaluation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.filter_contingencies"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.import_psse_case-Tuple{PSSERawData}","page":"API","text":"Convert the representable AC subset of parsed RAW data without dropping devices. Supplied tap ratios/phases and shunt BINIT remain fixed; automatic regulation is retained as RAW metadata, not inferred as droop or AVR. ZIP current loads, DC, FACTS, node/breaker topology, three-winding transformers and magnetizing models are rejected. Zero thermal ratings require an explicit unrated_limit in pu. The returned source maps preserve bus/ID and I/J/CKT identities; parsed operating points are retained separately from clamped generator initialization hints.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.import_psse_case"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.islanded_scenario_case-Tuple{Case, Contingency, ACState}","page":"API","text":"islanded_scenario_case(case, contingency, base_state)\n\nBuild the connected component to balance after a branch outage. The original reference-bus component is retained unless another component has strictly more buses. In that case the largest component is retained and the bus of its largest available generator becomes the new reference. Ties retain the original reference component. Discarded load and generation are reported.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.islanded_scenario_case"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.load_matpower_case-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"API","text":"load_matpower_case(path; base_frequency = 50.0, id = nothing)\n\nLoad the core bus, generator, load, and branch tables from a MATPOWER v2 case file. Values are normalized to the case base power. TAP=0 maps to unity; SHIFT is converted from degrees to radians. Nonzero bus GS/BS become aggregate fixed shunts with G=GS/baseMVA and B=BS/baseMVA. Other unsupported fields (including costs and angle-difference limits) remain outside this adapter. Fixed ratios and phase shifts are supported by AC evaluation and OPF/SCOPF.\n\nRATE_A == 0 is treated as an unavailable thermal limit and represented by a large finite limit so that the base OPF model remains numerically well-defined.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.load_matpower_case"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.load_psse_case-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"API","text":"Load the strict supported PSS/E RAW AC subset as a Case; use import_psse_case(parse_psse_raw(path)) for source identities and diagnostics. Unrated branches require an explicit unrated_limit (pu). Saved-state checking runs automatically and warns on failure; require_valid=true rejects a failed check. report_path optionally writes JSON. Use validation_options=(;...) for tolerances, or explicit check_saved_solution=false to disable checking. For report-only checks of unrated/unsupported files, use check_psse_file.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.load_psse_case"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.markdown_report-Tuple{DroopDesignMultiStartResult}","page":"API","text":"Render droop design parameter multi-start results as Markdown.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.markdown_report"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.markdown_report-Tuple{DroopSecurityResult}","page":"API","text":"Summarize contingency addition without converting unresolved cases into passes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.markdown_report"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.markdown_report-Tuple{EquilibriumReport}","page":"API","text":"Render an equilibrium report as compact Markdown for logs and artifacts.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.markdown_report"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.markdown_report-Tuple{SCOPFDiagnostics}","page":"API","text":"Render structured findings and breakpoint distances as Markdown.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.markdown_report"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.markdown_report-Tuple{SCOPFMultiStartResult}","page":"API","text":"Render every multi-start outcome and the comparison decision as Markdown.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.markdown_report"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.markdown_report-Tuple{SCOPFReport}","page":"API","text":"Render SCOPF status, tolerances, exact-curve checks, and scenario failures.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.markdown_report"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.mixed_reactive_controls-Tuple{Case}","page":"API","text":"Assign AVR to the largest fraction of online positive-Pmax generators.\n\nRound half upward by count; ties use ascending generator ID. ratings must explicitly supply positive system-base apparent ratings for every eligible unit. voltage_targets must supply an AVR target for each selected generator. Remaining online units retain their attached volt-var curves; offline records remain offline and receive FreeQ (no active control equation).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.mixed_reactive_controls"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.optimize_droop_multistart-Tuple{Study, Integer, AbstractDict}","page":"API","text":"optimize_droop_multistart(study, control_id, starts; ...)\n\nOptimize the same bounded droop design design from every named DroopSettings start. All attempts and exact-curve validation reports are retained. When omitted, reference_result is solved once and shared by every design run.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.optimize_droop_multistart"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.optimize_droop_parameters-Tuple{Study, AbstractVector{<:DroopControl}}","page":"API","text":"Optimize several shared droop curves using the normal smooth SCOPF builder.\n\nThe Study equipment and active-response policies are preserved. Explicit mixed assignments may design matching attached droop controls. Initial states are numerical starts only; independently replay the returned exact curves.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.optimize_droop_parameters"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.optimize_droop_parameters-Tuple{Study, Integer}","page":"API","text":"optimize_droop_parameters(study, control_id; slope_bounds, ...)\n\nOptimize one control's slope, voltage reference, and asymmetric deadband widths as variables shared by the base case and every training contingency. Omitted bounds fix that setting at its fixed-curve SCOPF value. The dispatch objective is unchanged, so fixing all bounds at the reference reproduces fixed-curve SCOPF. This is a smooth-NLP design problem for Ipopt or MadNLP; CCOpt may be used only after reconstructing the optimized fixed curve.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.optimize_droop_parameters"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.optimize_droop_security-Tuple{Study, AbstractVector{<:DroopControl}}","page":"API","text":"optimize_droop_security(study, controls; catalog=contingency_catalog(study.case), ...)\n\nIteratively add unresolved N-1 outages to shared droop design. Study contingencies are the initial training set and must use catalog identities. Every successful termination requires independent full AC assessment of all eligible catalog entries at the final settings/base. Excluded islands and catalog subsets cannot produce fulln1accepted. Errors, rejected masters and round limits are retained. The callback receives a complete immutable round after its decisions are made.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.optimize_droop_security"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.optimize_joint_design-Tuple{Case}","page":"API","text":"Optimize joint base-case controls; control_normalization=:bounds optionally uses unit-interval affine coordinates for free settings. droop_q_bounds=:implied omits generator-Q bounds already implied in exact arithmetic by the smoothed droop saturation and nested control capability. droop_q_formulation=:reduced substitutes that response into balance and objective expressions, eliminating the controlled-generator Q variable and droop equality. These options preserve the physical equations, objective and returned settings; their defaults preserve the original formulation. smooth_reactive_epsilon optionally fixes the absolute reactive smoothing width across controllers; nothing retains capability-scaled relative smoothing. It has no effect on the exact complementarity encoding.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.optimize_joint_design"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.optimize_multi_period-Tuple{MultiPeriodStudy}","page":"API","text":"Locally minimize duration-weighted active energy loss, with shared droop design and per-period AC dispatch coupled by explicit generator ramp rates. Equipment remains supplied and fixed in all periods. Uses sparse original-network AC.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.optimize_multi_period"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.optimize_multi_period_security-Tuple{Any}","page":"API","text":"Optimize shared droop across ordered periods and listed contingencies.\n\nresponse=:s2_proportional preserves supplied weights without saturation; :limit_aware uses the production response model. s2_emergency=true explicitly selects legacy comparison's ±0.05 pu voltage extension, 1.1 thermal ratings, 1% per-load DR, 10 MW shedding per outage, 10% combined relief, proportional reactive relief. Base cases always use original bounds/full load. Objectives are sequential: minimum total shedding, minimum total DR, then duration-weighted base loss. objective_policy=:reserve_loss inserts maximization of the minimum participating-generator upward-headroom fraction before loss; :reserve stops after the reserve stage. This is a dispatch preference, not an outage-security certificate. Equipment follows each Study policy; ramps link preventive dispatch only. Optional avr_bounds schedules preventive targets with movement in pu/hour; otherwise all supplied targets remain fixed. Optional reserve_generator_outages requires surviving participation to have upward room for each named generator's entire base output plus reserve_margin_pu, before any load relief. This explicit reserve target does not account for changed losses or replace exact outage AC. Retain this extra policy externally with the input studies for independent audit. objective_policy=:feasibility seeks a constrained witness without resource or loss optimization. Each initial-equipment entry may carry a scenarios dictionary of per-scenario taps/shunts starting values, falling back to its period entry. These starts do not change equipment bounds, timing, movement budgets or shared constraints. initial_avr_targets, initial_relief and initial_balancing_power retain the remaining primal variables across retries; each supplies one dictionary per period. Unknown scenario/target IDs, nonfinite values and inconsistent shared targets are rejected. AVR and individual relief starts are clipped to variable bounds. No manufacturer timing is inferred.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.optimize_multi_period_security"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.optimize_shunts-Tuple{Case, AbstractVector{ShuntControl}}","page":"API","text":"Optimize simple capacitor/reactor banks in base-case OPF; taps and droops stay fixed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.optimize_shunts"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.optimize_taps-Tuple{Case, AbstractVector{TapControl}}","page":"API","text":"Base-case AC OPF with explicitly selected continuous tap ratios.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.optimize_taps"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.parse_psse_raw-Tuple{IO}","page":"API","text":"Parse full-case PSS/E bus/branch RAW revisions 33, 34 and 36 from a path or IO. Known core records have typed fields; unmodeled sections remain opaque and intact. Errors name the source line. Omitted/empty optional fields remain absent, while text preserves comments, unknown fields, quoted identifiers and original units. Set encoding=:latin1 for legacy extended-ASCII files (default :utf8).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.parse_psse_raw"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.q_ref-Tuple{VoltVarDroop}","page":"API","text":"Preferred name for the reactive-power reference inside the voltage deadband.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.q_ref"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.qualify_droop_replacement-Tuple{Case, Case}","page":"API","text":"Qualify a proposed droop replacement before accepting it for independent-demand study analysis.\n\nThe source calibration point must replay both before and after replacement; optimization cannot repair a failed calibration check. Then both the original and replacement cases must have a witness at every required demand factor. All candidate curves are tested together. This does not certify shared SCOPF dispatch or preventive equipment settings across scenarios.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.qualify_droop_replacement"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.rank_contingencies-Tuple{ContingencyCatalog, Any}","page":"API","text":"rank_contingencies(catalog, report)\n\nRank the contingencies represented in an independently generated SCOPFReport. The ranking is an evaluation result, not a pre-solve filter: invalid and physical-only results remain visible and sort ahead of accepted results. Among accepted results, lower voltage, generator, or thermal margin means higher severity. A catalog entry absent from report is omitted rather than presumed safe.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.rank_contingencies"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.reactive_control_assignments-Tuple{Case}","page":"API","text":"Project a legacy droop-only case into one explicit mode per generator.\n\nGenerators without a legacy attachment become FreeQ; existing attachments become VoltVarDroop assignments. The case itself is not modified.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.reactive_control_assignments"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.reactive_limits-Tuple{Generator, Real}","page":"API","text":"Effective generator reactive interval at active output p, in system-base pu. The circle supplements, rather than replaces, the supplied rectangular bounds.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.reactive_limits"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.reactive_smoothing_epsilon-Tuple{VoltVarDroop, Real}","page":"API","text":"Return the reactive-power smoothing width for one droop control.\n\nThe relative default is expressed as a fraction of the smaller distance from the deadband reactive reference to either capability limit. This makes the regularization comparable for generators with different ratings and power bases.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.reactive_smoothing_epsilon"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.read_droop_design-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"API","text":"Read an droop design design; independently validate it against its study before use.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.read_droop_design"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.read_droop_slope_sweep-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"API","text":"Read a droop-slope sweep written by write_droop_slope_sweep.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.read_droop_slope_sweep"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.read_feasibility_result-Tuple{Any, FeasibilityProblem}","page":"API","text":"Load cached evidence only for an explicitly supplied matching problem, and replay it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.read_feasibility_result"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.read_scopf_result-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"API","text":"Load saved states and metadata. Revalidate them against the corresponding study before use.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.read_scopf_result"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.read_study-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"API","text":"Load a study and recheck case, outage, and response-policy validity.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.read_study"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.require_qualified_replacement-Tuple{DroopQualification}","page":"API","text":"Return the candidate only after every required condition has a revalidated witness.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.require_qualified_replacement"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.run_contingency_campaign-Tuple{AbstractString, Case, ACState}","page":"API","text":"run_contingency_campaign(directory, case, base_state; ...)\n\nRun the integrated pipeline with atomic per-outage JSON checkpoints. Resume requires identical case, catalog, base state, participation, screening/audit policy, solver settings, Julia version, source and dependency environment. Mismatched contracts are rejected without overwriting prior evidence. Corrupt checkpoints and evaluation errors are rerun; saved states are independently revalidated. A directory lock prevents concurrent writers. on_checkpoint is a progress callback after durable writes (and can interrupt the campaign). Returns (result, resumed_count, evaluated_count, fingerprint).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.run_contingency_campaign"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.scenario_case-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Case{T}, Contingency}} where T","page":"API","text":"Apply outages without changing the case, device order, or control settings.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.scenario_case"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.scopf_diagnostics-Tuple{Study, SCOPFResult}","page":"API","text":"scopf_diagnostics(study, result; breakpoint_tolerance=1e-3, binding_tolerance=1e-4, ...)\n\nIndependently validate a result, measure every active control's nearest-breakpoint distance, identify device-level binding limits, and select the contingency with the smallest independently recomputed physical margin. The critical-scenario score is a screening heuristic, not a dynamic stability ranking.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.scopf_diagnostics"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.scopf_operating_points-Tuple{Study, SCOPFResult}","page":"API","text":"Return all available-generator droop operating points grouped by scenario.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.scopf_operating_points"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.seed_global_feasibility!-Tuple{Any, ACState}","page":"API","text":"Map an AC point to every lifted variable and SOS2 weight, without changing the problem.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.seed_global_feasibility!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.shunt_powers-Tuple{ACNetwork, ACState}","page":"API","text":"Complex consumption at each fixed shunt, in network.shunts order. Capacitors have negative reactive consumption. Unavailable shunts return zero. This evaluator uses terminal voltage/current, independently of optimizer Ybus.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.shunt_powers"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.solve_global_feasibility-Tuple{FeasibilityProblem}","page":"API","text":"Run one explicitly budgeted global/SOC escalation and return its gate result and ledger.\n\nOnly independently replayed AC points can upgrade the gate here. Floating-point solver bounds are retained as solver_reported_infeasibility evidence, not promoted to independently verified certificates. This distinction also holds at time limits and when the solver reports OPTIMAL/INFEASIBLE.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.solve_global_feasibility"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.solve_opf_complementarity-Tuple{Case}","page":"API","text":"Solve an AC OPF with exact complementarity-encoded volt-var controls.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.solve_opf_complementarity"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.solve_opf_continuation-Tuple{Case}","page":"API","text":"solve_opf_continuation(case; smooth_epsilons, initial_state, kwargs...)\n\nSolve a sequence of smoothed OPF problems, passing each available state as the warm start for the next epsilon. The supplied epsilon sequence should usually decrease from a robust, relatively smooth value to the desired final value.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.solve_opf_continuation"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.solve_scopf-Tuple{Study}","page":"API","text":"solve_scopf(study; smooth_epsilon=1e-4, optimizer_factory=Ipopt.Optimizer, ...)\n\nSolve the base and all listed contingencies jointly, minimizing the base OPF base schedule-deviation objective. Contingencies impose feasibility constraints and have no additional cost. The fixed droop graph applies in every available unit. fixed_base_state evaluates security of an existing equilibrium without changing it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.solve_scopf"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.solve_scopf_continuation-Tuple{Study}","page":"API","text":"Solve successive smoothing widths, warm-starting all scenarios; stop on solver failure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.solve_scopf_continuation"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.solve_scopf_multistart-Tuple{Study, AbstractDict}","page":"API","text":"solve_scopf_multistart(study, starts; objective_atol=1e-7, objective_rtol=1e-6, ...)\n\nSolve and independently validate every named initial-state dictionary. A start may be empty for a cold solve. All runs are retained. Valid objectives are classified as :comparable, :divergent, :insufficient_valid_runs, or :no_valid_runs; the best run is identified only among independently valid results.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.solve_scopf_multistart"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.solver_operating_points-Tuple{Case, AbstractDict}","page":"API","text":"Return droop operating points grouped with the solver that produced them.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.solver_operating_points"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.sweep_droop_slope-Tuple{Study, Integer, Any}","page":"API","text":"Evaluate bounded fixed-slope fixed-curve SCOPF studies, anchored and warm-started at a reference slope.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.sweep_droop_slope"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.tap_design_metrics-Tuple{Case, TapOPFResult}","page":"API","text":"Raw physical and setting metrics; none implicitly adds an objective term.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.tap_design_metrics"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.validate_contingency_filter-Tuple{Case, ACState}","page":"API","text":"validate_contingency_filter(case, base_state; ...)\n\nCompare screening with exhaustive AC routes on the same catalog and base. Returns per-device-family counts of screened unresolved results, confirmed physical failures, and recall of unresolved outcomes at each ranking budget. An unresolved result includes solver rejection/error and is not a proof of infeasibility. Zero observed misses is evidence only for this input set.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.validate_contingency_filter"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.validate_droop_design-Tuple{Study, DroopDesignResult}","page":"API","text":"Independently replay an optimized design with an exact physical droop curve.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.validate_droop_design"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.validate_droop_design-Tuple{Study, SCOPFDroopDesignResult}","page":"API","text":"Check shared parameter bounds and independently replay exact SCOPF physics.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.validate_droop_design"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.validate_equilibrium-Tuple{Study, SCOPFResult}","page":"API","text":"validate_equilibrium(study, result; coupling_tolerance=1e-6, ...)\n\nRebuild each outage independently and check AC physics, exact droop replay, availability, and coupling to the base dispatch. Missing scenarios and states produce invalid reports. physical_valid is separate from solver success.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.validate_equilibrium"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.validate_feasibility_witness-Tuple{FeasibilityProblem, FeasibilityWitness}","page":"API","text":"Independently replay a witness against its frozen scope. Optimizer status is irrelevant.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.validate_feasibility_witness"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.validate_multi_period-Tuple{MultiPeriodStudy, MultiPeriodResult}","page":"API","text":"Independent original-network, exact-droop, shared-design, ramp, angle and energy-objective replay. Native convergence is required separately from physics.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.validate_multi_period"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.validate_multi_period_security-Tuple{Any, MultiPeriodSecurityResult}","page":"API","text":"Replay original AC physics, exact mixed controls, circles, response, relief, equipment bounds/timing, preventive ramps, angles and objective without solving.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.validate_multi_period_security"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.validate_psse_saved_solution-Tuple{PSSEImport}","page":"API","text":"Validate the voltages, angles and generator P/Q saved in RAW, without solving.\n\nReturns a JSON-compatible report separating AC balance, known operating limits, and missing thermal ratings. Default balance thresholds are 0.01 MW/MVAr per bus, explicitly allowing ordinary RAW export rounding; core equilibrium validation tolerances are unchanged. A pass covers the imported AC subset, not PSS/E convergence, automatic controls, dynamics or external branch-flow data.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.validate_psse_saved_solution"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.validate_reactive_assignments-Tuple{Case, AbstractVector{<:ReactiveControlAssignment}}","page":"API","text":"Validate explicit reactive-control assignments against one case.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.validate_reactive_assignments"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.validate_tap_design-Tuple{Case, TapOPFResult}","page":"API","text":"Separately report numerical termination, physical validity and tap-policy compliance.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.validate_tap_design"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.verified_feasibility_voltage_bounds-Tuple{FeasibilityProblem, FeasibilityResult}","page":"API","text":"Replay every LP weak-duality bound proof and return outward-rounded voltage bounds.\n\nThis never trusts the ledger's displayed bounds. Bounds from the original strict problem must not be reused as bounds on a voltage-relaxed problem.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.verified_feasibility_voltage_bounds"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.verify_feasibility_result-Tuple{FeasibilityProblem, FeasibilityResult}","page":"API","text":"Revalidate evidence and scope instead of trusting a stored status or fingerprint alone.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.verify_feasibility_result"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.with_bank_state-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{ShuntBank{T}, Any}} where T","page":"API","text":"Copy a bank with a supplied legal state/availability, leaving its design intact.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.with_bank_state"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.with_droop_settings-Tuple{Study, Integer, DroopSettings}","page":"API","text":"Return a copy of a study with one control replaced by optimized settings.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.with_droop_settings"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.with_droop_settings-Tuple{Study, SCOPFDroopDesignResult}","page":"API","text":"Reconstruct all shared curves while retaining the complete Study policy.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.with_droop_settings"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.with_joint_settings-Tuple{Case, JointDesignResult}","page":"API","text":"Reconstruct all physical settings for independent joint-design replay.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.with_joint_settings"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.with_shunt_settings-Tuple{Case, AbstractDict}","page":"API","text":"Physical replay of relaxed banks as equivalent fixed admittances; original bank metadata remain in the input case.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.with_shunt_settings"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.with_tap_settings-Tuple{Case, AbstractDict}","page":"API","text":"Reconstruct physical data at explicitly supplied ratios without changing the input.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.with_tap_settings"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.write_contingency_catalog-Tuple{AbstractString, ContingencyCatalog}","page":"API","text":"Write a structurally classified N-1 candidate catalog to JSON.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.write_contingency_catalog"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.write_contingency_pipeline-Tuple{AbstractString, ContingencyPipelineResult}","page":"API","text":"Write the complete routed contingency pipeline and its checks to JSON.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.write_contingency_pipeline"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.write_contingency_ranking-Tuple{AbstractString, AbstractVector{<:ContingencyRank}}","page":"API","text":"Write an exact-evaluation contingency ranking to JSON.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.write_contingency_ranking"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.write_droop_design-Tuple{AbstractString, DroopDesignResult}","page":"API","text":"Write an optimized droop design droop design and its training result to versioned JSON.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.write_droop_design"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.write_droop_design_comparison_plot-Tuple{AbstractString, Study, DroopDesignResult}","page":"API","text":"Write the reference and optimized exact droop curves with validation points.\n\nTraining operating points come from design.result. When both held-out keywords are supplied, non-base held-out points are added with a distinct marker colour. The input study must contain the reference settings used to create design.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.write_droop_design_comparison_plot"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.write_droop_design_multistart-Tuple{AbstractString, DroopDesignMultiStartResult}","page":"API","text":"Write droop design parameter-start inputs, outcomes, and spreads to versioned JSON.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.write_droop_design_multistart"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#DroopOPF.write_droop_plot","page":"API","text":"Write exact volt-var curves and operating points to a self-contained SVG.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.write_droop_plot"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.write_droop_qualification-Tuple{Any, DroopQualification}","page":"API","text":"Save the complete precursor qualification, including blocked conditions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.write_droop_qualification"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.write_droop_security-Tuple{AbstractString, Union{DroopSecurityResult, SCOPFDroopDesignResult}}","page":"API","text":"Persist shared droop settings, complete iteration evidence and final coverage.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.write_droop_security"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.write_droop_slope_sweep-Tuple{AbstractString, DroopSlopeSweep}","page":"API","text":"Write a versioned, machine-readable droop-slope sweep.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.write_droop_slope_sweep"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.write_droop_slope_sweep_plot-Tuple{AbstractString, DroopSlopeSweep}","page":"API","text":"Write a three-panel SVG of objective, voltage deviation, and branch loading.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.write_droop_slope_sweep_plot"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.write_feasibility_result-Tuple{Any, FeasibilityProblem, FeasibilityResult}","page":"API","text":"Save the full frozen problem, independently checked evidence and search attempts.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.write_feasibility_result"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.write_psse_validation_report-Tuple{AbstractString, AbstractDict}","page":"API","text":"Write a local JSON report. Nonfinite diagnostic values become JSON null.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.write_psse_validation_report"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.write_scopf_benchmark-Tuple{AbstractString, SCOPFBenchmarkReport}","page":"API","text":"Write reproducible SCOPF benchmark metadata and samples to versioned JSON.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.write_scopf_benchmark"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.write_scopf_branch_loading_plot-Tuple{AbstractString, Study, SCOPFResult}","page":"API","text":"Write branch loading percentages, marking unavailable branches as outages.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.write_scopf_branch_loading_plot"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.write_scopf_diagnostics-Tuple{AbstractString, SCOPFDiagnostics}","page":"API","text":"Write breakpoint distances and structured findings to versioned JSON.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.write_scopf_diagnostics"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.write_scopf_dispatch_plot-Tuple{AbstractString, Study, SCOPFResult}","page":"API","text":"Write active and reactive generator dispatch for every scenario.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.write_scopf_dispatch_plot"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.write_scopf_droop_plot-Tuple{AbstractString, Study, SCOPFResult}","page":"API","text":"Write exact droop curves with base and contingency operating points.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.write_scopf_droop_plot"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.write_scopf_evaluation_report-Tuple{AbstractString, Study, SCOPFResult}","page":"API","text":"Write a Markdown index connecting SCOPF inputs, outputs, plots, and validation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.write_scopf_evaluation_report"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.write_scopf_input_plot-Tuple{AbstractString, Study}","page":"API","text":"Plot declared outage, response, and equipment-policy inputs by scenario.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.write_scopf_input_plot"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.write_scopf_margin_plot-Tuple{AbstractString, Study, SCOPFResult}","page":"API","text":"Plot independently recomputed operating margins for every SCOPF scenario.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.write_scopf_margin_plot"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.write_scopf_multistart-Tuple{AbstractString, SCOPFMultiStartResult}","page":"API","text":"Write all multi-start outcomes and the objective-comparison classification.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.write_scopf_multistart"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.write_scopf_network_plot-Tuple{AbstractString, Study}","page":"API","text":"Write a topology-and-equipment diagram for the network declared in a SCOPF study.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.write_scopf_network_plot"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.write_scopf_report-Tuple{AbstractString, SCOPFReport}","page":"API","text":"Write a report to JSON; null margins mean an absent bound and null residuals are unavailable.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.write_scopf_report"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.write_scopf_residual_plot-Tuple{AbstractString, Study, SCOPFResult}","page":"API","text":"Write residual-to-tolerance ratios for AC balance, exact droop, and coupling.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.write_scopf_residual_plot"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.write_scopf_response_delta_plot-Tuple{AbstractString, Study, SCOPFResult}","page":"API","text":"Plot maximum base-to-scenario state changes on a logarithmic scale.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.write_scopf_response_delta_plot"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.write_scopf_result-Tuple{AbstractString, SCOPFResult}","page":"API","text":"Write scenario states and solver metadata to JSON. Nonfinite diagnostics become null.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.write_scopf_result"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.write_scopf_stage_assessment-Tuple{AbstractString, SCOPFStageAssessment}","page":"API","text":"Write a paired immediate/settled assessment immediate/settled assessment to JSON.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.write_scopf_stage_assessment"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.write_scopf_validation_matrix_plot-Tuple{AbstractString, Study, SCOPFResult}","page":"API","text":"Plot independent scenario checks, including invalid and corrupted results.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.write_scopf_validation_matrix_plot"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.write_scopf_validation_plots-Tuple{AbstractString, Study, SCOPFResult}","page":"API","text":"Write the complete SCOPF input/output validation bundle and return its file paths.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.write_scopf_validation_plots"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.write_scopf_voltage_plot-Tuple{AbstractString, Study, SCOPFResult}","page":"API","text":"Write bus-voltage trajectories and their limits for every scenario.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.write_scopf_voltage_plot"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.write_solver_comparison_plot-Tuple{AbstractString, Case, AbstractDict}","page":"API","text":"Write exact droop curves with operating points from several solvers.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.write_solver_comparison_plot"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.write_study-Tuple{AbstractString, Study}","page":"API","text":"Write a versioned study including its case and response policy to JSON.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.write_study"},{"category":"section","location":"first_opf/#Your-first-OPF:-build,-solve,-explain-and-check","page":"Your first OPF","text":"Goal: construct a two-bus network, connect one generator to a Volt/VAR curve, and explain the resulting operating point. Every @example block below runs when the documentation builds. The blocks share one Julia session.\n\nPrerequisites: installation and the meaning of active power P, reactive power Q, voltage magnitude v and voltage angle delta in concepts. All powers below are per unit on a 100 MVA system base.","title":"Your first OPF: build, solve, explain and check"},{"category":"section","location":"first_opf/#1.-Draw-the-physical-problem","page":"Your first OPF","text":"(Image: Two-bus teaching system with one generator, one branch, one consuming load and local voltage measurement.)\n\nFigure 1. Bus 1 fixes the angular reference and hosts generator 1. The controller measures bus 1 voltage. Bus 2 consumes 50 MW and 20 MVAr. There is no tap or shunt.\n\nThe branch resistance is r=001 pu, reactance is x=010 pu, and terminal apparent-power limit is 2 pu (200 MVA). Bus voltage bounds default to 0.9–1.1 pu. The reference flag fixes delta_1=0; it does not fix v_1=1 or grant unlimited generator output.\n\nusing DroopOPF\n\nnetwork = ACNetwork(\n    [Bus(1; reference=true), Bus(2)],\n    [Branch(11, 1, 2;\n        resistance=0.01, reactance=0.10, thermal_limit=2.0)],\n)\nload = Load(1, 2; p=0.50, q=0.20)\ngenerator = Generator(1, 1;\n    p_min=0.0, p_max=2.0, q_min=-1.0, q_max=1.0,\n    initial_p=0.50, initial_q=0.20)\n\nConstructor IDs identify devices: Load(1, 2; ...) means load ID 1 at bus ID 2. initial_p and initial_q are supplied generator reference values and numerical initialization information; they are not equality constraints on final output.","title":"1. Draw the physical problem"},{"category":"section","location":"first_opf/#2.-Give-the-generator-a-reactive-control-law","page":"Your first OPF","text":"The controller has reference v^ref=10 pu, lower/upper deadband edges v^-=099 and v^+=101 pu, deadband output q^0=0 pu and slope m=005 pu voltage per pu reactive power. The proportional gain is 1m=20 pu reactive power per pu voltage. The output is clipped to the supplied Q limits.\n\ncapability = ReactiveCapability(\n    p_min=0.0, p_max=2.0, q_min=-1.0, q_max=1.0)\nschedule = VoltageSchedule(1.0; v_db_low=0.99, v_db_high=1.01)\ncontrol = VoltVarDroop(schedule, 0.05, capability; q_ref=0.0)\nattachment = GeneratorControlAttachment(1, 1, RegulatedLocation(:bus, 1))\n\ncase = Case(\"teaching-two-bus\";\n    base_power=100.0, base_frequency=50.0, network=network,\n    loads=[load], generators=[generator], controls=[control],\n    attachments=[attachment])\n\nThe attachment joins generator ID 1 to control index 1, measuring bus 1. The Case keyword arguments name the power base (MVA), nominal frequency (Hz), network, loads, generators, controls and attachments. Nominal frequency does not introduce generator dynamics.","title":"2. Give the generator a reactive-control law"},{"category":"section","location":"first_opf/#3.-Solve-the-coupled-equilibrium","page":"Your first OPF","text":"The default base OPF minimizes\n\nJ=sum_ginmathcal Gleft(P_g-P_g^initial)^2\n      +10^-3(Q_g-Q_g^initial)^2right\n\nHere mathcal G is the generator set; P_gQ_g are active/reactive output, and the superscript initial denotes the supplied generator reference values. J is a weighted squared per-unit deviation, with no monetary units. The factor 10^-3 is the implemented relative Q weight. AC balance, voltage, capability, branch limits and the reactive-control law constrain the solution. This is not a fuel-cost or loss-minimization objective. With one generator and a fixed curve, this case has very little dispatch freedom; it teaches coupled equilibrium and replay. The security example adds a second generator and shared dispatch decisions.\n\nresult = solve_opf(case;\n    smooth_epsilon=1e-5,\n    optimizer_attributes=Dict(\"print_level\" => 0))\naudit = validate_equilibrium(case, result)\n@assert result.termination_status in (:LOCALLY_SOLVED, :ALMOST_LOCALLY_SOLVED, :OPTIMAL)\n@assert audit.valid\n\n(state_voltages_pu=round.(result.state.vm; digits=5),\n generator_P_MW=round.(100 .* result.state.pg; digits=3),\n generator_Q_MVAr=round.(100 .* result.state.qg; digits=3))\n(state_voltages_pu = [0.9784, 0.9508], generator_P_MW = [50.321], generator_Q_MVAr = [23.208])\n\nThe numerical smoothing width smooth_epsilon is in pu voltage. It approximates corners of the optimization encoding; the independent audit checks the exact physical curve. Smaller widths are not automatically easier to solve.","title":"3. Solve the coupled equilibrium"},{"category":"section","location":"first_opf/#4.-Explain-the-numbers-before-accepting-them","page":"Your first OPF","text":"There is only one generator. Its active injection must exceed the load's 050 pu by the branch's active loss. The reference-bus voltage is solved below the lower deadband edge so that the droop supplies positive reactive output. The downstream voltage also reflects the branch voltage drop.\n\nstate = result.state\nactive_loss_pu = sum(state.pg) - load.p\nexact_Q_pu = droop_response(control, state.vm[1]; p=state.pg[1])\n@assert active_loss_pu > 0\n@assert state.vm[1] < schedule.v_db_low\n@assert abs(state.qg[1] - exact_Q_pu) <= 1e-5\n\n(active_loss_MW=round(100 * active_loss_pu; digits=3),\n power_balance_residual=audit.power_balance_max,\n exact_droop_residual=audit.droop_residual_max)\n(active_loss_MW = 0.321, power_balance_residual = 2.0733414984874798e-14, exact_droop_residual = 7.771561172376096e-16)\n\nsum adds entries; abs takes absolute value; exact_Q_pu is the curve response at the solved measured voltage and active output. A small residual measures agreement with an equation, not closeness to zero generation or zero losses.","title":"4. Explain the numbers before accepting them"},{"category":"section","location":"first_opf/#5.-Check-that-the-validator-can-reject-a-wrong-point","page":"Your first OPF","text":"Deliberately change reactive generation by 0.05 pu (5 MVAr) without changing voltages. This violates the frozen equilibrium; the solver is not called again.\n\nbad_qg = copy(state.qg)\nbad_qg[1] += 0.05\nbad_state = ACState(copy(state.vm), copy(state.va), copy(state.pg), bad_qg)\nbad_audit = validate_equilibrium(case, bad_state)\n@assert !bad_audit.valid\n\n(original_valid=audit.valid, corrupted_valid=bad_audit.valid)\n(original_valid = true, corrupted_valid = false)","title":"5. Check that the validator can reject a wrong point"},{"category":"section","location":"first_opf/#6.-What-to-change-next","page":"Your first OPF","text":"Evaluate droop_response(control, 0.97; p=0.5) by hand using the Volt/VAR tutorial. It should return 0.4 pu reactive power.\nIncrease load reactive demand and observe the upstream/downstream voltages; solve and validate each changed case afresh.\nCompare this generator with FreeQ, FixedQ or AVR through explicit reactive assignments.\nBefore adding an outage, decide what is shared and allowed to respond.\n\nThis one-point result establishes neither outage security nor transient stability. The explicit construction and corruption check make it a reproducible starting point for your own model.","title":"6. What to change next"},{"category":"section","location":"mixed_avr_droop/#Mixed-AVR-and-Volt/VAR-controls","page":"Mixed AVR / droop","text":"A single network may combine free reactive output, fixed schedules, automatic voltage regulation (AVR) and Volt/VAR droop. The reactive-mode primer explains the distinctions. Every available generator still obeys AC balance and its supplied capability.\n\n(Image: Reactive modes and the equation each adds to AC balance.)\n\nFigure 1. AVR adjusts reactive output to a voltage target until capability binds; a droop follows its supplied curve. These are steady-state relations.","title":"Mixed AVR and Volt/VAR controls"},{"category":"section","location":"mixed_avr_droop/#Apparent-rating-and-effective-reactive-limits","page":"Mixed AVR / droop","text":"Generator(...; s_rated=S) declares apparent rating S in pu on the case system base. Let PQ be active/reactive output in pu and Q^minQ^max supplied box limits. An explicit rating adds P^2+Q^2le S^2 without replacing those bounds. For Ple S, the effective reactive interval intersects the box with -sqrtS^2-P^2sqrtS^2-P^2. cdot is absolute value and the square root is nonnegative. reactive_limits(generator, p) evaluates this intersection at active output p.\n\nOmitting s_rated preserves the rectangular capability model. PSS/E MBASE is not automatically a verified nameplate rating. An apparent-power circle is not a complete voltage-dependent converter current envelope.","title":"Apparent rating and effective reactive limits"},{"category":"section","location":"mixed_avr_droop/#Voltage-regulation-and-sharing","page":"Mixed AVR / droop","text":"An AVR holds its measured-voltage target while its effective Q interval permits. At a reactive limit it releases voltage regulation. A clipped droop retains its parameters and delivers the effective Q bound. Multiple AVR units at one regulated location require a consistent target and share the same normalized position within their current reactive intervals.\n\nReactiveControlAssignment makes the mode and regulated location explicit. Pass the same assignments to the solve and independent validation. Legacy attachments and explicit assignments follow their documented compatibility rules; unsupported combinations are rejected rather than silently ignored.\n\nmixed_reactive_controls(case; avr_fraction, ratings, voltage_targets) can select online positive-Pmax units deterministically by descending Pmax and ascending ID, rounding the requested count to nearest integer with half upward. Offline records stay offline. Supply ratings and targets explicitly; the helper does not infer manufacturer capability or controller dynamics.","title":"Voltage regulation and sharing"},{"category":"section","location":"mixed_avr_droop/#Shared-design-and-chronological-security","page":"Mixed AVR / droop","text":"The smooth optimize_joint_design, optimize_droop_parameters and optimize_multi_period paths support compatible explicit mixed assignments and selected free legacy curve parameters. A selected curve must match its assignment. Consult solver compatibility for exact parameter-design restrictions.\n\noptimize_multi_period_security couples chronological base dispatch with ramp rates, shared curves and declared outage recourse. Each period's preventive AVR target remains shared across its outages. Optional target bounds and hourly movement limits must be supplied explicitly. Fixed equipment stays fixed unless selected through the study's equipment policy.\n\nThe production response is limit-aware, with normal operating bounds and full load unless the caller explicitly chooses another supported contract. Relief, emergency limits and alternate response policies must not be inferred from a solver setting or from this tutorial. Preserve the complete study contract.","title":"Shared design and chronological security"},{"category":"section","location":"mixed_avr_droop/#Optional-headroom-objective","page":"Mixed AVR / droop","text":"The usual base loss objective is not a fuel-cost curve. An optional reserve-aware policy first maximizes a common normalized upward-headroom variable h before optional loss polishing. For generator g, let P_g be base active output and L_gU_g its participating effective active interval, all in pu. The constraint is P_g+(U_g-L_g)hle U_g. Variable h is dimensionless.\n\nobjective_policy=:reserve stops after the reserve stage; :reserve_loss adds loss polishing; the usual default remains :loss. Headroom is a dispatch preference, not a guarantee of delivering reserve through every outage or a substitute for security constraints.","title":"Optional headroom objective"},{"category":"section","location":"mixed_avr_droop/#Independent-checks-and-model-limits","page":"Mixed AVR / droop","text":"Require native success and validate_multi_period_security(studies, result).valid. Replay checks every period/state, exact controls, capability, equipment, response, ramps, target sharing and the declared objective. Store the input studies as well as the result; a result's policy fields are not an independent specification.\n\nAVR/droop labels do not instantiate synchronous-machine, governor, PLL, grid-forming or grid-following dynamics. Steady-state security does not establish frequency response, transient stability, discrete movement or a dynamic transition.","title":"Independent checks and model limits"},{"category":"section","location":"volt_var/#Reading-and-designing-a-Volt/VAR-curve","page":"Read a Volt/VAR curve","text":"A Volt/VAR controller supplies a relation between measured voltage and generator reactive output. Here VAR means reactive power; the code uses per-unit reactive power on the case system base. Start with the worked OPF if you want to see how the curve enters a network solve.\n\n(Image: A saturated Volt/VAR curve with an explicit deadband, slope convention, Q limits and voltage breakpoints.)\n\nFigure 1. Illustrative exact curve: reference 1.0 pu, edges 0.99/1.01 pu, zero deadband output, slope 0.05 pu voltage/pu reactive power, Q limits −1/+1 pu. Positive output injects reactive power. The chart is a control law, not a trajectory.","title":"Reading and designing a Volt/VAR curve"},{"category":"section","location":"volt_var/#Define-every-parameter","page":"Read a Volt/VAR curve","text":"Symbol Meaning Figure value\nv^m Measured voltage magnitude, pu Horizontal coordinate\nv^ref Reference voltage, pu 1.0\nd^-d^+ Lower/upper deadband widths, pu voltage 0.01 each\nv^-=v^ref-d^-, v^+=v^ref+d^+ Deadband edges, pu voltage 0.99, 1.01\nq^0 Reactive output inside the deadband, pu 0\nm Positive voltage-per-reactive-power slope 0.05\nq^raw Unsaturated reactive request, pu Computed below\nQ^-Q^+ Effective reactive lower/upper limits, pu −1, +1\nQ Delivered equilibrium reactive output, pu Vertical coordinate\n\nDefine the positive part z_+=max(z0) and clipping operatornameclip(zLU)=min(Umax(Lz)). Here z is a real input, and LU its lower/upper bounds in the same units. The exact law is\n\nq^raw(v^m)=q^0+fracv^- -v^m_+m\n                  -fracv^m-v^+_+mqquad\nQ=operatornameclip(q^rawQ^-Q^+)\n\nInside the deadband both hinge terms vanish. Below its lower edge the controller injects more reactive power; above its upper edge it absorbs more. Clipping prevents a request outside capability from becoming a delivered output. In a proportional segment the derivative is dQdv^m=-1m. Therefore smaller positive slope means a stronger reactive response; the stored slope is not that derivative or its magnitude.","title":"Define every parameter"},{"category":"section","location":"volt_var/#Calculate-one-point-by-hand","page":"Read a Volt/VAR curve","text":"With the figure's parameters, a measured voltage of v^m=097 pu gives q^raw=(099-097)005=04 pu, and clipping leaves Q=04 pu. At v^m=090 pu the raw request is 1.8 pu, so the delivered output is 1 pu. On a 100 MVA base those outputs are 40 and 100 MVAr, respectively.\n\nusing DroopOPF\nschedule = VoltageSchedule(1.0; v_db_low=0.99, v_db_high=1.01)\ncap = ReactiveCapability(p_min=0.0, p_max=2.0, q_min=-1.0, q_max=1.0)\ncurve = VoltVarDroop(schedule, 0.05, cap; q_ref=0.0)\n@assert isapprox(droop_response(curve, 0.97; p=0.5), 0.4; atol=1e-12)\n@assert droop_response(curve, 0.90; p=0.5) == 1.0\n[(voltage=v, reactive_output=droop_response(curve, v; p=0.5))\n for v in (0.90, 0.97, 1.00, 1.03, 1.10)]\n5-element Vector{@NamedTuple{voltage::Float64, reactive_output::Float64}}:\n (voltage = 0.9, reactive_output = 1.0)\n (voltage = 0.97, reactive_output = 0.4)\n (voltage = 1.0, reactive_output = 0.0)\n (voltage = 1.03, reactive_output = -0.4)\n (voltage = 1.1, reactive_output = -1.0)\n\nA deadband does not hold voltage at its center. It holds the requested reactive output constant while the network equations determine voltage.","title":"Calculate one point by hand"},{"category":"section","location":"volt_var/#Capability-can-depend-on-active-output","page":"Read a Volt/VAR curve","text":"If a generator has an explicitly supplied apparent rating S^rated, active output P leaves at most sqrt(S^rated)^2-P^2 reactive magnitude, provided Ple S^rated. That circle intersects the supplied Q box and any attached control capability. Here P is active output, S^rated is apparent rating, and cdot is absolute value. All powers use the same system base.\n\nIncreasing active output can therefore clip the same curve at a different Q limit. A clipped voltage-target AVR releases regulation; a clipped droop retains its parameters and delivers the available limit. See mixed controls. A circle is not a full voltage-dependent converter-current model.","title":"Capability can depend on active output"},{"category":"section","location":"volt_var/#Design-a-parameter,-then-freeze-the-result","page":"Read a Volt/VAR curve","text":"A fixed-curve OPF solves for the operating point on supplied curves. Droop design makes selected curve parameters additional optimization variables inside explicit bounds. A reference shift translates the deadband edges if its widths are fixed; slope changes alter proportional gain. Widths may be asymmetric. The reactive deadband output and capability remain supplied in the standard design family.\n\nUse the bounded design API for a small parameter study, or shared contingency design for one controller across training outages. Held-out conditions must use the frozen design according to the declared dispatch/equipment policy. Retuning for each evaluation outage tests a different policy.","title":"Design a parameter, then freeze the result"},{"category":"section","location":"volt_var/#Smooth-encoding-versus-the-exact-law","page":"Read a Volt/VAR curve","text":"Smooth nonlinear programming (NLP) replaces the positive-part corners with smooth approximations. Exact complementarity introduces inequalities and orthogonality conditions to describe the same physical graph. Both need independent validation. Numerical smoothing width, physical deadband width and acceptance tolerance are three different quantities. See solver formulations.","title":"Smooth encoding versus the exact law"},{"category":"section","location":"data_model/#Data-model","page":"Data model reference","text":"","title":"Data model"},{"category":"section","location":"data_model/#Read-the-data-as-an-electrical-model","page":"Data model reference","text":"Start with the complete two-bus construction, then AC branch/shunt physics. This page is the detailed constructor, control and serialization reference. Notation and units defines system bases, current directions and mathematical aliases used below.\n\n(Image: Electrical and measurement connections of a complete two-bus Case.)\n\nFigure 1. A Case combines network, devices and control attachments. The measured bus and the generator injection bus are separate pieces of data, even when equal.","title":"Read the data as an electrical model"},{"category":"section","location":"data_model/#Network-and-operating-devices","page":"Data model reference","text":"Bus, Branch, Load, and ACNetwork describe the balanced AC network in per-unit quantities. A Generator is attached to a network bus and carries active/reactive limits, availability, and initial values.\n\nnetwork = ACNetwork(\n    [Bus(1; reference = true), Bus(2)],\n    [Branch(1, 1, 2; resistance = 0.01, reactance = 0.1,\n            thermal_limit = 5.0)],\n)\ngenerator = Generator(\n    1, 1;\n    p_min = 0.0, p_max = 2.0,\n    q_min = -1.0, q_max = 1.0,\n    initial_p = 0.5,\n)","title":"Network and operating devices"},{"category":"section","location":"data_model/#Volt-var-droops","page":"Data model reference","text":"VoltageSchedule stores the reference voltage and deadband. VoltVarDroop adds a positive voltage-per-reactive-power slope, a reactive reference inside the deadband, and a ReactiveCapability.\n\ncontrol = VoltVarDroop(\n    VoltageSchedule(1.0; v_db_low = 0.99, v_db_high = 1.01),\n    0.05,\n    0.0,\n    ReactiveCapability(p_min = 0.0, p_max = 2.0,\n                       q_min = -1.0, q_max = 1.0),\n)\n\nThe exact curve is available through droop_curve(control) and evaluated with droop_response(control, voltage; p = active_power). The active-power range on the control is enforced for its attached generator.\n\nq_ref(control) is the preferred name for the constant reactive reference inside the voltage deadband. The legacy q_at_deadband field remains available for backward compatibility. New code may use the keyword constructor:\n\ncontrol = VoltVarDroop(schedule, slope, capability; q_ref = 0.0)","title":"Volt-var droops"},{"category":"section","location":"data_model/#Explicit-reactive-control-modes","page":"Data model reference","text":"FreeQ, FixedQ, AVR, and VoltVarDroop are explicit ReactiveControlModes. A ReactiveControlAssignment associates one mode with one generator; AVR and Volt–VAr modes also require a regulated location. validate_reactive_assignments rejects duplicate generator assignments, out-of-range fixed-Q schedules and AVR setpoints, unknown locations, and droop capabilities incompatible with generator limits.\n\nassignments = [\n    ReactiveControlAssignment(1, AVR(1.02), RegulatedLocation(:bus, 1)),\n    ReactiveControlAssignment(2, FixedQ(0.1)),\n]\nvalidate_reactive_assignments(case, assignments)\n\nreactive_control_assignments(case) gives every generator in a legacy case an explicit mode without modifying the case: attached droops remain VoltVarDroop, and unattached generators become FreeQ. The existing solver and study-file representation remains backward compatible.\n\nFor base-case smooth or CCOpt OPF, pass reactive_assignments=assignments to solve_opf or solve_opf_complementarity, respectively, and to validate_equilibrium. This list replaces legacy attachments for that call; omitted generators have bounded, free Q. FixedQ adds a Q schedule equality and the independent validator reports :fixed_q when its error exceeds power_tolerance. Unavailable generators retain zero output. AVR is supported by both the smooth and CCOpt base-case entry points. Study JSON v8 and SCOPF result JSON v4 store optional assignments explicitly, along with equipment policy equipment controls, timing, movement budgets, and scenario settings. Readers preserve nothing for older documents, which retains legacy attachment semantics. Restored assignments are revalidated and SCOPF validation rejects a study/result assignment mismatch. Joint design accepts the same optional assignments for base-case tap/shunt design, validates the returned equilibrium against them, and persists them in joint-design schema v2. Explicit assignments cannot be combined with legacy droop-parameter design variables in one run. When assignments contain only FreeQ/FixedQ, there are no complementarity constraints and CCOpt delegates to MadNLP; these runs are not independent third-solver evidence. Explicit VoltVarDroop assignments retain the exact complementarity encoding.","title":"Explicit reactive-control modes"},{"category":"section","location":"data_model/#Attachments","page":"Data model reference","text":"GeneratorControlAttachment connects one generator to one control and gives the regulated location. The base OPF permits one static attachment per generator.\n\nattachment = GeneratorControlAttachment(\n    1, 1, RegulatedLocation(:bus, 1),\n)\n\nThe attachment separates the injection bus from the regulated measurement location.","title":"Attachments"},{"category":"section","location":"data_model/#Transformer-data-contract","page":"Data model reference","text":"Branch accepts explicit kind=:line or kind=:transformer, plus tap_ratio=1 and phase_shift=0. The complex tap is on the declared from side: a = tap_ratio * cis(phase_shift). Ratio is dimensionless, finite and strictly positive; phase is finite and in radians. Existing keyword and typed positional constructors retain unity/zero defaults. Network/case conversion, control attachment and contingency copies preserve both settings.\n\nMATPOWER TAP=0 becomes unity and marks a line; a supplied TAP (including unity) or SHIFT marks a transformer. SHIFT is converted once from degrees to radians. No physical tap bounds or legal positions are inferred from those fields. The MATPOWER importer converts nonzero bus GS/BS as aggregate fixed shunts (see below).\n\nStudy schema v2 requires both transformer settings; v3 adds an explicit fixed-shunt list; v4 adds banks. The reader migrates line-only v1 studies to unity/zero and rejects contradictory transformer metadata labelled v1. Unchanged result/report document kinds retain schema v1. Older readers reject v2, rather than silently losing transformer physics.\n\nStudy schema v9 introduced the explicit line/transformer kind; schema v10 also identifies limit-aware automatic active response. Supported older uncoupled or corrective studies reload with kind=:line when kind metadata is absent. Historical coupled preventive studies require original-source replay rather than silent migration to a different active-response policy.\n\nThe AC model supports fixed ratios and fixed phase shifts in AC evaluation, OPF, SCOPF and existing bounded droop design. Unavailable branches contribute no current while retaining supplied settings. Tap optimization, AVR and switched shunts are documented below and in mixed controls.","title":"Transformer data contract"},{"category":"section","location":"data_model/#Fixed-transformer-electrical-model","page":"Data model reference","text":"In this section r and x are series resistance/reactance; j is the imaginary unit; b is total charging susceptance; tau is positive tap ratio; phi is phase shift in radians; and a is the complex tap. Vf/Vt are endpoint voltage phasors, Uf is internal from-side voltage, J is series current, and If/It are currents into the branch. Sf/St are terminal complex powers. Yff, Yft, Ytf, Ytt are the branch's two-port admittance entries (first letter: current endpoint; second: voltage endpoint). conj denotes conjugation. The pi schematic and derivation show their physical locations.\n\nFor series admittance y = 1/(r + j*x), total branch charging b, and from-side complex tap a = tau * exp(j*phi), the optimizer assembles:\n\nYff = (y + j*b/2) / |a|²       Yft = -y / conj(a)\nYtf = -y / a                  Ytt = y + j*b/2\n\nIt applies |Sf| <= rating and |St| <= rating separately. Both the smooth JuMP formulation (Ipopt/MadNLP) and complementarity formulation (CCOpt) use the same terminal-limit builder, including transformer-outage exclusion. Phase shifts make the off-diagonal entries different; they must not be symmetrized.\n\nPhysical validation follows a separate calculation. With Uf = Vf/a and J = y*(Uf - Vt), it computes If = (J + j*b/2*Uf)/conj(a) and It = -J + j*b/2*Vt, then Sf = Vf*conj(If) and St = Vt*conj(It). Bus balances sum these terminal powers rather than reuse optimizer Ybus. Both terminal powers are positive into the branch; their sum is net branch absorption. Charging is included once at each internal terminal, with the from-side contribution transformed by the tap.\n\nValidate the reconstructed network with both-terminal currents and exact control replay. Tap/shunt settings are steady-state inputs unless explicitly selected for bounded optimization; no three-winding dynamics are inferred.","title":"Fixed transformer electrical model"},{"category":"section","location":"data_model/#Fixed-bus-shunts","page":"Data model reference","text":"FixedShunt is network equipment, separate from Load and branch charging:\n\nshunt = FixedShunt(8, 10; conductance=0.02, susceptance=0.1, available=true)\nnetwork = ACNetwork([Bus(10; reference=true)], Branch[]; shunts=[shunt])\n\nIDs and bus references must be positive; IDs are unique within the shunt list. Admittance is finite and in per unit on the case base. Conductance G must be nonnegative; susceptance B may have either sign. Positive B is capacitive. Here G/B denote shunt conductance/susceptance, V is its bus-voltage phasor, S is complex consumption, and j is the imaginary unit. Complex terminal consumption is S = (G - j*B)*|V|²: positive P consumes active power, negative Q injects reactive power. Several distinct devices may share a bus and their contributions sum once. available=false contributes zero while preserving supplied data. An empty list preserves the previous network model.\n\nThe optimizer adds G+j*B to the bus diagonal in Ybus. The independent evaluator shunt_powers(network, state) computes V*conj((G+j*B)*V) per device in network.shunts order. power_balance sums these powers alongside branch terminal powers. Bus shunts do not enter branch_flows or duplicate line charging.\n\nMATPOWER GS is MW consumed at unit voltage; BS is MVAr injected at unit voltage. Import divides both by baseMVA, with no sign reversal: G=GS/baseMVA, B=BS/baseMVA. Nonzero aggregate rows become one fixed record using the bus ID as shunt ID; zero rows add none. No bank identity, legal step, nominal schedule or automatic controller is inferred. Negative GS is rejected because this passive equipment model does not represent active-power injection.\n\nStudy JSON v3 requires network.shunts; each record includes ID, bus ID, G, B and availability. Readers retain v1/v2 support with empty shunts and reject nonempty shunts misleadingly labelled with an older schema. New writers use v4 even when the list is empty, so old readers fail explicitly. Other document kinds keep their existing versions. Numeric promotion, case/control copies and existing branch/generator contingency overlays preserve fixed shunts. Dedicated shunt contingencies are not part of this fixed-shunt representation.","title":"Fixed bus shunts"},{"category":"section","location":"data_model/#Supplied-switched-bank-states","page":"Data model reference","text":"bank = ShuntBank(201, 30;\n    step_susceptances=(0.02, -0.01),\n    step_conductances=(0.001, 0.0005),\n    legal_states=((0,0), (1,0), (2,0), (0,1), (1,1)),\n    state=(1,0), nominal_state=(1,0))\nchanged = with_bank_state(bank, (2,0))\nnetwork = ACNetwork(buses, branches; shunts=fixed_shunts, banks=[changed])\n\nEach state is a tuple of nonnegative integer counts for the corresponding step admittances. Legal combinations must be explicitly supplied, nonempty and unique; both current and nominal states must be legal even when the bank is unavailable. Metadata are copied into immutable tuples. Unavailability produces zero admittance without erasing the installed state. bank_admittance returns aggregate G+jB; bank_powers independently computes consumption P+jQ by summing step currents. Positive B is capacitive, so Q is negative consumption. Fixed shunts and banks share a unique ID namespace. Do not represent the same physical aggregate in both. MATPOWER GS/BS remains fixed admittance and does not infer bank identities or states.\n\nStudy schema v4 requires network.banks and preserves all metadata. Schema v5 adds optional reactive-control assignments; schema v6 adds branch_id to a branch-terminal location; schema v7 adds selected continuous equipment and its SCOPF timing policy; schema v8 adds optional corrective movement-limit maps. Readers migrate v1-v3 to empty banks and reject nonempty banks mislabeled as old schemas. Existing branch/generator contingencies preserve supplied bank states. The model uses fixed states in OPF, preventive/corrective SCOPF and droop design. Selected equipment may be optimized separately; a bank state is not an automatic switching policy or trajectory.","title":"Supplied switched-bank states"},{"category":"section","location":"data_model/#Continuous-transformer-tap-design","page":"Data model reference","text":"policy = [TapControl(11; lower=0.95, upper=1.05, nominal=1.0)]\nresult = optimize_taps(case, policy; initial_state=state, smooth_epsilon=1e-5)\nreport = validate_tap_design(case, result)\nphysical_case = with_tap_settings(case, result.taps)\nmetrics = tap_design_metrics(case, result)\nwrite_tap_design(\"tap-design.json\", result)\nreloaded = read_tap_design(\"tap-design.json\")\n\nBounds are required; no range is inferred from imported taps. Branch IDs must be unique, known and available. Omitted branches remain at their supplied ratios. Set lower == upper to fix a selected device, and supply initial if the supplied ratio is outside the bounds. Initial values are numerical starts; nominal is a positive reference for reporting, defaulting to the supplied ratio, and need not lie in the optimization envelope. Neither introduces a penalty in the objective.\n\nThe baseline objective remains generator dispatch deviation. Phase shifts, shunts and droop curves stay fixed. Ratios enter nonlinear balances and both-terminal thermal constraints; input case data remain unchanged. Results are continuous relaxations, without discrete-position recovery or switching-count interpretation. report.physical evaluates the reconstructed physical network; policy_valid separately checks bounds and fixed settings. A failed solve can have no state or ratios; physical validation is then unavailable and overall validity is false.\n\nThe dedicated base-case entry point accepts a Case. The equipment-aware SCOPF interface also accepts the same controls on Study, with explicit scenario policy coupling through SCOPFControlPolicy; see SCOPF. The current implementation uses the smooth encoding with Ipopt by default and supports the existing optimizer-factory interface. See tap design evidence for sweeps and comparisons.","title":"Continuous transformer tap design"},{"category":"section","location":"data_model/#Simple-continuous-shunt-design","page":"Data model reference","text":"bank = ShuntBank(201, 30; step_susceptances=(0.02,),\n    step_conductances=(0.001,), legal_states=((0,), (1,), (2,), (3,)), state=(1,))\n# After adding bank to case.network.banks:\npolicy = [ShuntControl(201)] # B bounds default to [0, 0.06] pu\nresult = optimize_shunts(case, policy; initial_state=state)\nreport = validate_shunt_design(case, result)\nmetrics = shunt_design_metrics(case, result)\nphysical_case = with_shunt_settings(case, result.susceptances)\nwrite_shunt_design(\"shunt-design.json\", result)\nreloaded = read_shunt_design(\"shunt-design.json\")\n\nA reactor uses negative step B, e.g. −0.02 gives an interval [−0.06, 0]. Here B is the aggregate susceptance and Bstep the susceptance per section, both in pu. Conductance G is tied to B through their common fractional count; here G=0.001(B/Bstep). Banks with multiple step types or zero step B are rejected when selected, as are unavailable or unknown banks and fixed-shunt IDs. Unselected heterogeneous banks remain fixed.\n\nOptional lower, upper, initial and nominal are all B in pu. Bounds must lie in the legal-count envelope; equal bounds fix B. The default initial B is the supplied bank setting, so narrowing bounds past it requires an explicit start. The default nominal B comes from the bank's nominal state. Nominal B must lie in the physical envelope but may lie outside a narrower optimization interval.\n\nTaps, phase shifts and droop settings stay fixed; multiple simple banks can be selected independently. The baseline dispatch objective remains unchanged. Metrics include fractional count, B deviations, G, active consumption, reactive injection and branch losses. The result is explicitly continuous, not a recovered switching position. with_shunt_settings creates an evaluation case by replacing selected banks with equivalent fixed shunts; the original case retains bank metadata. Policy bounds are checked separately by validate_shunt_design. JSON stores settings and policy; retain the input study alongside it for reproducible replay.\n\nValidation evidence includes capacitor/reactor sweeps and plots. Heterogeneous optimization is post-scaling backlog; the gate requires evidence that simple banks, transformers and droops scale adequately first.","title":"Simple continuous shunt design"},{"category":"section","location":"data_model/#Joint-equipment-and-droop-design","page":"Data model reference","text":"result = optimize_joint_design(case;\n    tap_controls=[TapControl(11; lower=0.95, upper=1.05)],\n    shunt_controls=[ShuntControl(201)],\n    droop_controls=[DroopControl(2; slope_bounds=(0.04, 0.10))],\n    initial_state=state)\nreport = validate_joint_design(case, result)\nphysical_case = with_joint_settings(case, result)\nmetrics = joint_design_metrics(case, result)\nwrite_joint_design(\"joint-design.json\", result)\nreloaded = read_joint_design(\"joint-design.json\")\n\nOmit a control list to keep that family fixed; select devices independently within each list. DroopControl additionally accepts v_ref_bounds, deadband_low_bounds, deadband_high_bounds and initial::DroopSettings. Missing bounds fix a parameter to its supplied value. Bounds preserve the positive-slope, positive-reference and positive-total-deadband contract. No free-knot curve family is introduced. Multiple attached available droop controls may be selected.\n\nThe objective is identical across fixed/free configurations. Reports distinguish solver success, parameter/equipment policy compliance and reconstructed physical validity. Ratios and bank settings remain continuous relaxations. See equipment design results and visualizations for the complete grid, multi-start variability, objective components and matched conditional benefits.","title":"Joint equipment and droop design"},{"category":"section","location":"first_security/#Your-first-security-study","page":"Your first security study","text":"Goal: use one preventive dispatch and fixed curves for a base state, a line outage and a generator outage. This complete example runs during the documentation build. Read the single-state example and the policy tutorial first.","title":"Your first security study"},{"category":"section","location":"first_security/#1.-Use-a-network-that-has-alternative-supply-paths","page":"Your first security study","text":"(Image: Triangle network with generators 7 and 9 at buses 10 and 20 and a load at bus 30; branches 11, 22 and 33 provide alternative paths.)\n\nFigure 1. Removing branch 22 still leaves a supply path to bus 30. Removing generator 9 leaves generator 7 as the only active source.\n\nThe synthetic network is a triangle: branches 11 (bus 10–20), 22 (20–30) and 33 (10–30). Generator 7 injects at bus 10, generator 9 at bus 20, and the load consumes at bus 30. Bus 10 is the angle reference. IDs are intentionally nonconsecutive.\n\nusing DroopOPF\nnetwork = ACNetwork(\n    [Bus(10; reference=true), Bus(20), Bus(30)],\n    [Branch(id, f, t; resistance=0.01, reactance=0.10, thermal_limit=2.0)\n     for (id, f, t) in [(11,10,20), (22,20,30), (33,10,30)]])\ngenerators = [Generator(id, bus;\n    p_min=0.0, p_max=1.2, q_min=-1.0, q_max=1.0,\n    initial_p=0.3, initial_q=0.0)\n    for (id, bus) in [(7,10), (9,20)]]\ncap = ReactiveCapability(p_min=0.0, p_max=1.2, q_min=-1.0, q_max=1.0)\ncontrols = [VoltVarDroop(\n    VoltageSchedule(1.0; v_db_low=0.99, v_db_high=1.01), m, cap; q_ref=0.0)\n    for m in [0.05, 0.075]]\nattachments = [GeneratorControlAttachment(g.id, i, RegulatedLocation(:bus, g.bus_id))\n    for (i, g) in enumerate(generators)]\ncase = Case(\"teaching-security\";\n    base_power=100.0, base_frequency=50.0, network=network,\n    loads=[Load(1, 30; p=0.6, q=0.15)], generators=generators,\n    controls=controls, attachments=attachments)\n\nEach generator's limits are 0–1.2 pu active power and −1–1 pu reactive power. The load consumes 60 MW and 15 MVAr on the 100 MVA base. The comprehension variable m is a positive droop slope in pu voltage/pu reactive power. enumerate supplies the array control index i separately from generator ID g.id. No equipment design variables or corrective tap/shunt actions are selected.","title":"1. Use a network that has alternative supply paths"},{"category":"section","location":"first_security/#2.-State-the-outages-and-response-policy","page":"Your first security study","text":"study = Study(case;\n    contingencies=[Contingency(:line_22; branch_ids=[22]),\n                   Contingency(:generator_9; generator_ids=[9])],\n    participation=default_participation(case))\nresult = solve_scopf(study;\n    smooth_epsilon=1e-5,\n    optimizer_attributes=Dict(\"print_level\" => 0))\nreport = equilibrium_report(study, result)\n@assert report.valid\nsort!(collect(keys(result.states)); by=string)\n3-element Vector{Symbol}:\n :base\n :generator_9\n :line_22\n\nStudy defaults to preventive active dispatch with limit-aware automatic response. default_participation uses effective active-capability ranges; survivor weights are normalized for each outage. Curves stay fixed. Each surviving reactive controller responds to its scenario's measured voltage.\n\n(Image: Shared curves and policy with separate base and outage AC states.)\n\nFigure 2. In this example the two outage boxes are :line_22 and :generator_9. Each has its own voltages and outputs; the base dispatch is not independently redesigned for each box.\n\nThe solver objective penalizes deviation of base generator outputs from their supplied references, as defined in the first OPF. Outage states add feasibility requirements. This is not a sum of independently optimized base cases.","title":"2. State the outages and response policy"},{"category":"section","location":"first_security/#3.-Inspect-what-the-generator-outage-actually-changes","page":"Your first security study","text":"base = result.states[:base]\noutage = result.states[:generator_9]\n@assert abs(outage.pg[2]) <= 1e-8\n@assert abs(outage.qg[2]) <= 1e-8\n@assert outage.pg[1] > base.pg[1]\n\n(base_P_MW=round.(100 .* base.pg; digits=3),\n outage_P_MW=round.(100 .* outage.pg; digits=3),\n surviving_unit_increase_MW=round(100 * (outage.pg[1]-base.pg[1]); digits=3))\n(base_P_MW = [30.101, 30.101], outage_P_MW = [60.273, 0.0], surviving_unit_increase_MW = 30.172)\n\nState vectors use generator order [7,9], so position 2 belongs to generator 9. The outaged unit has zero P/Q; its droop equation is inactive. Generator 7 must supply the load plus the changed network loss within its unchanged capability. The angle-reference bus does not provide additional hidden generation.","title":"3. Inspect what the generator outage actually changes"},{"category":"section","location":"first_security/#4.-Test-outage-availability-independently","page":"Your first security study","text":"corrupted = deepcopy(result)\ncorrupted.states[:generator_9].qg[2] = 0.1\ncorrupted_report = equilibrium_report(study, corrupted)\n@assert !corrupted_report.valid\n@assert :unavailable_generator in corrupted_report.violations[:generator_9]\n\n(original_valid=report.valid, corrupted_valid=corrupted_report.valid)\n(original_valid = true, corrupted_valid = false)\n\nThe intentionally restored 0.1 pu reactive output is 10 MVAr from a failed unit. Rejection is necessary even if some other bounds appear satisfactory.","title":"4. Test outage availability independently"},{"category":"section","location":"first_security/#5.-Describe-coverage-honestly","page":"Your first security study","text":"This example checks two selected outages, not the full N-1 catalogue. Use contingency_catalog(case) to enumerate available device losses and inspect eligibility/islanding before deciding the required coverage. A successful small study is not evidence about unlisted outages, larger systems, discrete movement or transient stability.\n\nTo design a shared curve next, use bounded parameter design. To add omitted outages iteratively, use contingency addition, which still requires complete final assessment of the declared eligible catalogue.","title":"5. Describe coverage honestly"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#Installation-and-learning-paths","page":"Installation and learning paths","text":"","title":"Installation and learning paths"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#Install-Julia-and-the-package","page":"Installation and learning paths","text":"Use Julia 1.10 or newer. A Julia session shows a julia> prompt; the following is Julia code, entered there or saved in a .jl script:\n\nimport Pkg\nPkg.activate(\"droopopf-tutorial\"; shared=true)\nPkg.add(url=\"https://github.com/frederikgeth/DroopOPF.jl.git\", rev=\"v0.10.0-rc1\")\nusing DroopOPF\n\nThe named environment keeps the tutorial's dependencies together. The revision pins the documented prerelease; use a specific revision for reproducible studies. Package installation requires network access. Ipopt and the other declared Julia dependencies are installed with the package; teaching examples require no additional input files.\n\nIf you already have a checkout, run this shell command from its root:\n\njulia --project=. -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.instantiate()'\n\nThen start julia --project=. for an interactive session in that environment. Pkg.instantiate() installs the dependencies described by the project/manifest; it does not run an optimization campaign.","title":"Install Julia and the package"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#Choose-a-path","page":"Installation and learning paths","text":"Background or task Suggested sequence\nNew to power-system optimization Concepts → first OPF → Volt/VAR → AC model\nFamiliar with AC OPF; new to autonomous controls Volt/VAR → reactive modes → study workflows\nReady for controller design First OPF → droop optimization → droop security\nImporting a saved operating point Units and signs → PSS/E → validation\nInvestigating an unsuccessful solve Validation → solvers → feasibility → robustness\n\nThe first OPF constructs every object explicitly; it contains no pre-existing case variable or hidden input file. Copy its blocks in order into one Julia session.","title":"Choose a path"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#Check-your-installation","page":"Installation and learning paths","text":"For a checkout, this shell command loads a bundled synthetic two-bus MATPOWER fixture, solves it and independently checks its equilibrium:\n\njulia --project=. examples/release_smoke.jl\n\nThat smoke case uses the control assignments provided by its importer. The teaching case instead explicitly attaches a Volt/VAR curve so you can see its role.","title":"Check your installation"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#Symbols,-units-and-scope","page":"Installation and learning paths","text":"Before transferring equipment data, read notation and units. Power fields use the case system base, branch phase angles use radians, and Volt/VAR slopes use voltage change per reactive-power change. A droop slope is not a reactive-power-per-voltage gain.\n\nNext: understand what the optimizer chooses, or go directly to the complete worked example.","title":"Symbols, units and scope"},{"category":"section","location":"multi_period/#Chronological-multi-period-design","page":"Multi-period design","text":"The initial multi-period capability solves one deterministic sequence of AC operating periods in a single sparse nonlinear model. A single set of reactive voltage-droop parameters is shared across all periods. Each period has its own active/reactive dispatch and voltage state. Equipment stays at the supplied fixed settings throughout the horizon.\n\nThis API covers deterministic base-operation periods with fixed equipment. Storage, commitment and dynamic trajectories are outside this formulation.\n\n(Image: Chronological base dispatch and alternative outage states, with shared curves.)\n\nFigure 1. The base-operation API models the upper row. Outage rows belong to the separate multi-period security extension. Horizontal arrows are preventive ramps, not dynamic response trajectories. See the workflow tutorial.","title":"Chronological multi-period design"},{"category":"section","location":"multi_period/#Time-and-objective-contract","page":"Multi-period design","text":"Cases must share the same ordered network, equipment settings, generators, controls, attachments, power base, frequency and load identities. Only load powers may vary. All original voltage, thermal and generator limits remain active, with optional explicit branch-angle bounds.\n\nFor period t, duration_hours[t] is both its energy weight and the elapsed start-to-start time to period t+1. The last duration weights energy only. There is no initial or terminal ramp boundary, cyclic closure or interpolated trajectory. Inputs represent period operating points with a piecewise-constant energy accounting convention.\n\nThe objective is the sum of duration_hours[t] * base_power * (sum(pg[t]) - sum(load_p[t])), in MWh. This is active network energy loss, including shunt conductance losses. It is not generation cost or a storage energy balance.\n\nFor generator g, ramp_limits[g] = (up, down) specifies nonnegative rates in pu/hour on the case power base. Between consecutive period starts:\n\n-r_g^downDelta t le P_gt+1-P_gt le r_g^upDelta t\n\nIn this equation g is generator ID, t is chronological period index, P_gt is preventive active output in pu, r_g^upr_g^down are nonnegative ramp rates in pu/hour and Delta t is the elapsed start-to-start interval in hours. The lower bound limits a decrease; the upper limits an increase. The global notation reference uses k for time to avoid confusing it with a branch's to-side endpoint t.\n\nOmitted generators have no inter-period ramp restriction. Zero rates freeze active output across periods. These planned dispatch ramps do not replace the library's automatic contingency-response policy and do not establish dynamic frequency performance.","title":"Time and objective contract"},{"category":"section","location":"multi_period/#API","page":"Multi-period design","text":"study = MultiPeriodStudy(period_cases;\n    duration_hours=[1., 1., 2.],\n    ramp_limits=Dict(generator_id => (0.2, 0.1)),\n    angle_limits=Dict(branch_id => (-0.5, 0.5)))\nresult = optimize_multi_period(study;\n    droop_controls=[DroopControl(1;\n        slope_bounds=(0.02, 0.08), v_ref_bounds=(0.98, 1.02))],\n    initial_states=period_initial_states)\naudit = validate_multi_period(study, result)\n\nSlope bounds are in physical pu-voltage / pu-reactive-power units, not percent. Omitted droop fields remain fixed. Empty droop_controls fixes all curves. The first implementation uses the existing smooth volt-var family; the normal independent validator also checks exact PWL droop residuals. Native convergence, all AC states, design bounds, ramp constraints, branch-angle constraints, period losses and the total energy objective must pass independently. Solver-reported local optima do not establish global optimality or design uniqueness.","title":"API"},{"category":"section","location":"multi_period/#Mixed-AVR-and-contingency-recourse-extension","page":"Multi-period design","text":"The base-operation API above retains its fixed-equipment scope. The separate optimize_multi_period_security API supports mixed AVR/droop controls, apparent capability circles, outage recourse, explicit Study equipment policies and optional preventive AVR scheduling. See Mixed AVR and droop studies for the model contract, declared legacy comparison comparison settings and qualification limits.","title":"Mixed AVR and contingency recourse extension"},{"category":"section","location":"validation/#Can-I-trust-the-operating-point?","page":"Validate an operating point","text":"A solver can terminate successfully on its numerical encoding while the returned state misses an exact physical curve or a declared policy. Conversely, a useful physical point does not turn a timed-out solve into native convergence. Record both kinds of evidence.\n\n(Image: Solver formulation and independent replay are separate stages before accepting an operating point.)\n\nFigure 1. Physical replay reconstructs branch currents, nodal balance, controls and limits. The study validator additionally checks cross-state policy.","title":"Can I trust the operating point?"},{"category":"section","location":"validation/#Check-the-right-object","page":"Validate an operating point","text":"Result being checked Validator Additional meaning\nBase OPF state/result validate_equilibrium(case, result) AC equations, supplied controls and device limits.\nSelected tap/shunt/droop design Its validate_*_design function Reconstructed physical settings and declared design bounds.\nCoupled security study validate_equilibrium(study, result) Base/outage states plus response, sharing and equipment timing.\nBase multi-period design validate_multi_period(study, result) Every period, shared curves, ramps, angle restrictions and energy objective.\nMulti-period security validate_multi_period_security(studies, result) Period/scenario policy, physical replay and temporal coupling.\n\nThe first OPF is a complete executable example, including a reactive-output corruption that the validator rejects. The following is an API fragment: case and result must already come from the same solved problem.\n\nvalidation = validate_equilibrium(case, result)\nvalidation.valid\nvalidation.power_balance_max\nvalidation.droop_residual_max\n\nreport = equilibrium_report(case, result)\nprintln(markdown_report(report))\n\nFor base OPF, validation.valid describes physical checks; inspect native status as well. equilibrium_report combines numerical metadata with independent replay. Passing the result instead of only result.state also carries its smoothing metadata. If you used explicit reactive_assignments, pass the same assignments to the relevant validator; a validator must check the problem you actually solved.","title":"Check the right object"},{"category":"section","location":"validation/#Read-residuals-and-margins-differently","page":"Validate an operating point","text":"A residual measures disagreement with an equality: zero is the target. A margin measures distance from a bound: a negative margin means a violation. For a supplied upper limit u and value z, margin is u-z; for a lower limit l, margin is z-l. Here ulz share the units of the checked quantity.\n\nCheck What is recomputed Why it matters\nActive/reactive balance Generator injection minus load, shunt and branch consumption A visually plausible voltage profile can still violate conservation.\nExact control relation Delivered Q versus the exact supplied droop, AVR or fixed-Q relation Smooth solver equations and physical corners differ.\nVoltage/capability bounds Limits on each available device/state, including supplied apparent ratings A reference bus has no unlimited reserve.\nTerminal loading Apparent power at both branch ends Charging and losses make the ends different.\nAvailability Zero injection/flow for outaged devices Removing a control equation is not enough if its injection remains.\nPolicy coupling Shared settings and declared base-to-outage/period movement Separate feasible points may belong to an impermissible policy.","title":"Read residuals and margins differently"},{"category":"section","location":"validation/#Use-the-actual-default-tolerances","page":"Validate an operating point","text":"The base validate_equilibrium defaults below are implementation defaults, not manufacturer data or permission to loosen a study's previously declared gates.\n\nKeyword Default Checked unit\npower_tolerance 1e-6 pu active/reactive power residual\ndroop_tolerance 1e-5 pu reactive-power residual\nlimit_tolerance 1e-6 Native pu unit of each device margin\nunavailable_tolerance 1e-8 pu unavailable-device output\navr_voltage_tolerance 1e-5 pu measured-voltage residual\navr_sharing_tolerance 1e-5 Dimensionless normalized Q-position mismatch\n\nSmoothing widths belong to the numerical encoding. Tolerances belong to the acceptance check. Capability bounds belong to the physical contract. Changing one must not be described as changing another. Check the specific validator/API for additional policy and complementarity tolerances.","title":"Use the actual default tolerances"},{"category":"section","location":"validation/#An-unsuccessful-search-has-several-possible-meanings","page":"Validate an operating point","text":"Accepted witness: a returned point passes the applicable native, physical and policy gates for its declared coverage.\nRejected physical point: replay identifies a particular violated equation or bound.\nUnresolved numerical solve: a timeout, error or failed local search supplies no proof that a valid operating point cannot exist.\nCertified infeasibility: an independently replayable contradiction excludes the declared feasible set; see feasibility qualification and global certificates.\n\nFor controller replacement, use the feasibility precursor to freeze the equipment freedoms, source angle constraints and acceptance contract. For repeated-start behavior and interpretation of local solutions, read robustness. Describe the required outage/period coverage explicitly when reporting results.","title":"An unsuccessful search has several possible meanings"},{"category":"section","location":"ac_model/#Following-power-through-the-AC-model","page":"AC physics and schematics","text":"Goal: connect the electrical schematic, equations and data fields. The model is a balanced, positive-sequence AC equivalent. It does not represent individual phase conductors, electromagnetic transients or dynamic controllers.","title":"Following power through the AC model"},{"category":"section","location":"ac_model/#Voltage,-current-and-complex-power","page":"AC physics and schematics","text":"At bus b, let v_b0 be voltage magnitude in pu and delta_b voltage angle in radians. The complex voltage phasor is widetilde V_b=v_bexp(mathrm jdelta_b), where mathrm j^2=-1 and exp is the exponential function. For a terminal current I directed out of the bus into equipment, complex power is S=widetilde Voverline I=P+mathrm jQ. The bar denotes complex conjugation; P is active power and Q reactive power, both in pu on the system base.\n\nA positive generator output injects into the bus. A positive load consumes from the bus. Branch terminal powers below are positive into the branch. These conventions make nodal balance an injection-minus-consumption equation.","title":"Voltage, current and complex power"},{"category":"section","location":"ac_model/#One-branch,-including-a-transformer","page":"AC physics and schematics","text":"(Image: Branch pi equivalent showing the complex tap on the declared from side, series impedance, half charging at internal terminals and both inward terminal currents.)\n\nFigure 1. The ideal complex tap acts on the from side. Charging uses the internal from-side voltage after that tap. Both endpoint ratings must be checked.\n\nFor branch ell from bus f to bus t, define:\n\nSymbol Definition Native field\nrx Series resistance/reactance, pu resistance, reactance\nz=r+mathrm jx; y=1z Series impedance; admittance, pu Calculated\nb^ch Total charging susceptance, pu charging\ntau0; phi Tap magnitude; phase shift, radians tap_ratio, phase_shift\na=tauexp(mathrm jphi) Complex from-side tap Calculated\nU_f=widetilde V_fa Voltage on the internal from terminal Calculated\nI_fI_t Currents into the branch from its endpoint buses Calculated\nJ_s Series current from the internal from node to the to node Calculated\n\nThe independent current calculation is\n\nbeginaligned\nJ_s = y(U_f-widetilde V_t)\nI_f = fracJ_s+mathrm j(b^ch2)U_foverline a\nI_t = -J_s+mathrm j(b^ch2)widetilde V_t\nS_f = widetilde V_foverlineI_fqquad\nS_t = widetilde V_toverlineI_t\nendaligned\n\nHere S_fS_t are complex powers into the branch. Their sum is net complex branch absorption; it need not be zero. Re(S_f+S_t) is active loss, where Re takes the real part. Charging can make reactive absorption negative. S_fleoverline S_ell and S_tleoverline S_ell separately enforce the branch's supplied terminal rating overline S_ell. Magnitude is denoted by cdot. The two terminal flows can differ, especially with charging.\n\nThe optimizer assembles a nodal admittance matrix Y from the same physical data. It uses bus power widetilde V_boverline(Ywidetilde V)_b, where (Ywidetilde V)_b is net current leaving bus b. Validation separately sums branch-terminal powers rather than reusing that optimizer expression. See the electrical data contract.","title":"One branch, including a transformer"},{"category":"section","location":"ac_model/#Shunts-consume-or-supply-power-according-to-voltage","page":"AC physics and schematics","text":"For shunt ID s at bus b, conductance G_sge0 and susceptance B_s are in pu. Its complex consumption is\n\nS_s^sh=(G_s-mathrm jB_s)v_b^2\n\nThus positive G_s consumes active power; positive B_s supplies reactive power because reactive consumption is negative. For example, B_s=002 pu and v_b=1 inject 0.02 pu reactive power, or 2 MVAr on a 100 MVA base. At v_b=095, that injection is 002(095)^2=001805 pu. A constant-Q load does not scale with v_b^2 in this way.\n\nA FixedShunt fixes admittance. A ShuntBank also records supplied section/legal state metadata. Selected bank optimization may be a continuous relaxation; its optimized fractional count is not automatically a legal switching action. Do not represent the same physical capacitor in both a load adjustment and a shunt.","title":"Shunts consume or supply power according to voltage"},{"category":"section","location":"ac_model/#Nodal-power-balance","page":"AC physics and schematics","text":"Let mathcal G_b be generators, mathcal L_b loads, mathcal E_b incident branch terminals and mathcal S_b shunts/banks at bus b. Let S_g=P_g+mathrm jQ_g be generator injection; S_l^L load consumption; S_ellb^term branch power into branch ell at b; and S_s^sh shunt consumption. Then the complex nodal residual r_b is\n\nr_b=sum_ginmathcal G_bS_g\n    -sum_linmathcal L_bS_l^L\n    -sum_ellinmathcal E_bS_ellb^term\n    -sum_sinmathcal S_bS_s^sh=0\n\nBoth the real and imaginary parts must vanish within their acceptance tolerance. These are active and reactive balance, respectively. Summing real balances over buses gives generation minus load equal to active branch and shunt losses.","title":"Nodal power balance"},{"category":"section","location":"ac_model/#Units-and-reference-choices-are-physical-modeling-choices","page":"AC physics and schematics","text":"Case.base_power is the common power base in MVA. Native per-unit constructor inputs must already be converted consistently; notation and units provides conversion formulas. A tap ratio is dimensionless, phase shift is radians, and shunt susceptance is admittance in pu.\n\nOne angle reference removes an arbitrary common angle offset. It is not a source of unbounded active output. Generator availability and P/Q capability remain explicit. Missing import ratings cannot be replaced by a demonstration placeholder and then described as verified equipment limits; see PSS/E import.\n\nNext: how the same model is coupled across outages and periods.","title":"Units and reference choices are physical modeling choices"},{"category":"section","location":"#DroopOPF.jl","page":"Home","text":"Find a steady-state AC operating point that respects the network and its reactive controls; then check that point independently. DroopOPF supports optimal power flow (OPF), shared Volt/VAR design and security-constrained OPF (SCOPF).\n\n(Image: The path from network data and an explicit control policy to a solve and independent physical replay.)\n\nFigure 1. A numerical solve produces a candidate. Acceptance also needs independent replay of the exact network, control laws, device limits and declared policy.","title":"DroopOPF.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Learn-with-one-small-network","page":"Home","text":"Install and run: set up Julia and choose a learning path.\nUnderstand the problem: distinguish dispatch, automatic response and controller design before choosing an API.\nBuild your first OPF: a complete two-bus example, executed when these docs build, from device data to a deliberately corrupted result.\nRead a Volt/VAR curve: work through deadband, slope and saturation.\nFollow AC power through a branch: current directions, taps, shunts, per-unit bases and nodal balance.\nSolve a security study: execute a complete two-outage example and reject injection from a failed generator.\nAdd security or time: understand what is shared and what changes across outages, demand samples and chronological periods.\n\nNotation and units defines the mathematical symbols and their code counterparts. Individual teaching pages also define symbols where they first appear. All examples use a balanced, positive-sequence AC model. An equilibrium or an hourly schedule is not a transient-stability or device-switching trajectory assessment.","title":"Learn with one small network"},{"category":"section","location":"#Find-the-guide-for-your-task","page":"Home","text":"Your question Start here Detailed reference\nHow do I assemble network and control data? First OPF Data model, PSS/E import\nWhy does reactive output change with voltage? Volt/VAR tutorial Mixed AVR/droop\nWhich controls may change after an outage? Study workflows SCOPF policy\nHow do I choose one curve for many conditions? Design versus response Droop optimization, contingency addition\nHow are demand samples different from time periods? Scenario/time diagram Multi-period model\nCan I trust this result? Validation Feasibility, robustness\nWhich model features are supported? Supported scope API reference","title":"Find the guide for your task"},{"category":"section","location":"#Implementation-reference","page":"Home","text":"Use the API for signatures, supported scope for model limitations and development guide to build or test the package. The teaching structure takes inspiration from the concept primers, executed examples and validation guidance in BMOPFTools.jl.","title":"Implementation reference"},{"category":"section","location":"notation/#Notation,-signs-and-units","page":"Notation, signs and units","text":"Symbols are defined locally in teaching pages. This reference brings them together and maps the mathematical quantities to the API. Older technical pages sometimes use another spelling; those aliases are listed explicitly below.","title":"Notation, signs and units"},{"category":"section","location":"notation/#Devices,-conditions-and-states","page":"Notation, signs and units","text":"Symbol Meaning\nb; mathcal B Bus index; set of buses.\ng; mathcal G; mathcal G_b Generator index; set of generators; generators connected to bus b.\nell; ft Branch index; its declared from and to buses. In branch equations t is an endpoint, not a time index.\nj; mathcal J Controller index; controller set. In complex-number expressions, the roman symbol mathrm j=sqrt-1 is the imaginary unit.\nc; mathcal C; c=0 Contingency/scenario index; declared scenario set; base state.\nk; mathcal T; Delta t_k Chronological period index; ordered period set; period duration/start-to-start interval in hours. Older pages use t for the period index.\nx; x_c; x_kc AC state (vdeltaPQ); state in scenario c; state in period k, scenario c. This is a steady-state vector.\ntheta Selected controller/equipment design parameters; not a bus angle.\n\nDevice IDs are positive identifiers, while state vectors use case ordering. A subscript identifies a device or condition, not a new multiplication.","title":"Devices, conditions and states"},{"category":"section","location":"notation/#Electrical-quantities","page":"Notation, signs and units","text":"Symbol Meaning and unit API field / convention\nS_base System three-phase apparent-power base, MVA case.base_power\nf_base Nominal frequency, Hz case.base_frequency; not a frequency-dynamics model\nv_bdelta_b Bus voltage magnitude, pu; voltage angle, rad vm, va\nwidetilde V_b=v_b e^mathrm jdelta_b Complex bus voltage phasor, pu Reconstructed from vm, va; technical branch pages also write Vf, Vt.\nP_gQ_g Generator active/reactive injection, pu pg, qg; positive means injected into the network.\nP_b^LQ_b^L Aggregate active/reactive consumption at a bus, pu Sum of Load.p, Load.q; positive means consumption.\nS=P+mathrm jQ Complex power, pu ``\nI_fI_t Currents into a branch at its two terminals, pu Both point from their bus into the branch.\nS_fS_t Complex powers into a branch, pu S_f=widetilde V_foverlineI_f, likewise at t.\nr_ellx_ellz_elly_ell Series resistance, reactance, impedance and admittance, pu z_ell=r_ell+mathrm jx_ell, y_ell=1z_ell.\nb_ell^ch Total branch charging susceptance, pu Branch.charging; half at each internal terminal.\ntau_ellphi_ella_ell Positive tap ratio; phase shift, rad; complex tap tap_ratio, phase_shift; a_ell=tau_ell e^mathrm jphi_ell on the from side.\nG_sB_s Bus-shunt conductance/susceptance, pu Positive G_s consumes active power; positive B_s injects reactive power.\nY Complex nodal admittance matrix, pu Maps bus phasors to net outgoing current.\nunderline zoverline z or z^minz^max Lower/upper bound on a real quantity z Context supplies its units; a bar on a complex phasor/current instead means conjugation.\n\nComplex conjugation is also written conj(z) or z^*; conjugate transpose is (cdot)^H or, in the global-feasibility page, (cdot)^* on a vector/matrix. z denotes absolute value or complex magnitude. z_infty is the largest absolute component of a vector. sum sums the indexed quantities.","title":"Electrical quantities"},{"category":"section","location":"notation/#Reactive-control-and-design","page":"Notation, signs and units","text":"Symbol Definition\nv^m_jc Voltage magnitude measured by controller j in scenario c; it may differ from the voltage at the generator bus.\nv_j^ref; d_j^-d_j^+ Voltage reference; nonnegative lower/upper deadband widths, pu voltage.\nv_j^-=v_j^ref-d_j^-; v_j^+=v_j^ref+d_j^+ Lower/upper deadband edges, pu voltage; code v_db_low, v_db_high.\nm_j Positive droop slope in pu voltage per pu reactive power; code slope. The proportional gain magnitude is 1m_j.\nq_j^0 or Q_j^0 Reactive output inside the deadband, pu; code q_ref, legacy q_at_deadband. Superscript 0 here is a curve parameter, not the base scenario.\nq_j^raw Unsaturated response of a controller, pu reactive power.\nQ_g^-(P)Q_g^+(P) Effective reactive limits at active output P, including supplied capability restrictions.\nS_g^rated Optional generator apparent rating, pu on the system base; code s_rated.\nz_+=max(z0) Positive-part function, also called a hinge.\noperatornameclip(zLU)=min(Umax(Lz)) Projection of z into the real interval LU.\nepsilon_vepsilon_Q Numerical smoothing widths for voltage/reactive response; not deadband widths or physical-limit relaxations.\n\nThe droop-security page writes V_jc for measured voltage, v_j for the reference and Q_jc for the attached generator's reactive output. Its slope m_j has the same convention as above. The solver guide's Qraw, Qref, Vref, V, slope are respectively q^rawq^0v^refv^mm.","title":"Reactive control and design"},{"category":"section","location":"notation/#Response,-time-and-objectives","page":"Notation, signs and units","text":"Symbol Definition\nP_g0, P_gc Base/outage active output of generator g, pu.\nalpha_gc Nonnegative response weight normalized over eligible survivors in c; dimensionless.\nlambda_c Common active-response request signal, pu active power; code balancing_power. After clipping, it need not equal total delivery.\nL_gcU_gc Effective active lower/upper bounds after capability and declared movement restrictions, pu.\nr_g Symmetric base-to-outage redispatch allowance, pu; not a rate.\nr_g^upr_g^down Planned inter-period active-output ramp rates, pu/hour.\nP_g^initialQ_g^initial Supplied generator reference outputs in the default dispatch-deviation objective, pu.\nJ Optimization objective, with units specific to the selected objective.\nE_loss Duration-weighted active network energy loss, MWh.\nh Dimensionless minimum normalized upward headroom in reserve-aware studies.","title":"Response, time and objectives"},{"category":"section","location":"notation/#Per-unit-conversion-and-signs:-a-worked-check","page":"Notation, signs and units","text":"Per unit (pu) is a physical quantity divided by its declared base. On a 100 MVA power base, 50 MW active demand is 050 pu and 20 MVAr reactive demand is 020 pu. Generator Q=+020 pu injects 20 MVAr; load q=+020 pu consumes 20 MVAr. A capacitive shunt injects reactive power, so its reactive consumption is negative.\n\nFor physical voltage/impedance conversion, let V_base be line-to-line base voltage in kV. Then Z_base=V_base^2S_base is impedance base in ohms. z_pu=z_ohmZ_base and Y_base=1Z_base. A compatible three-phase current base is I_base=S_base(sqrt3 V_base) in kA. The per-unit network constructors expect already consistent bases; base_frequency is not a voltage base.\n\nAngles supplied to native constructors use radians. Thus 30 degrees equals pi6 radians, where pi is the circle constant. Importers handle their documented source conventions; do not convert an angle twice.\n\nIf a percentage droop is defined over a full reactive range Delta Q=Q^max-Q^min, a voltage span of 4% gives m=004Delta Q. For Delta Q=2 pu, m=002 pu voltage/pu reactive power. Always identify the span and power base before using a percentage from another source.","title":"Per-unit conversion and signs: a worked check"},{"category":"section","location":"notation/#Abbreviations-used-in-the-reference-pages","page":"Notation, signs and units","text":"Abbreviation Meaning\nAC / DC Alternating current / direct current. The DC power-flow screening approximation estimates active flows; it is not an exact AC equilibrium.\nOPF / SCOPF Optimal power flow / security-constrained optimal power flow.\nAVR / AGC Automatic voltage regulation / automatic generation control.\nPWL / NLP Piecewise linear / nonlinear program (or nonlinear programming).\nLP / SOC / PSD Linear program / second-order cone / positive semidefinite.\nKKT Karush–Kuhn–Tucker optimality conditions; used to encode interval projection in complementarity formulations.\nLODF Line-outage distribution factor, used in approximate active-flow screening.\nMW / MVAr / MVA / MWh Megawatt / megavar / megavoltampere / megawatt-hour.\npu Per unit: divided by the declared physical base.\nID Device or scenario identifier; not necessarily its vector index.\n\nUse local symbols on the global-feasibility page for its certificate matrices: it deliberately uses a separate LP decision vector.","title":"Abbreviations used in the reference pages"}]
}
